window.SCOUT_DATA = {"meta": {"title": "Jordan My Tools upgrade scout", "upgrade_count": 28, "digest_count": 33, "priority_counts": {"P0": 6, "P1": 10, "P2": 9, "P3": 3}}, "upgrades": [{"id": "U-001", "title": "Per-client Context OS pack layout (context/sources/skills/work/releases)", "priority": "P0", "theme": "Client context, dossier & artifact federation", "summary": "Standardize every client pack as a filesystem Context OS: context/ (ICP, audience, positioning, product, voice), sources/ (transcripts, evidence), skills/, work/, releases/, plus agent rulebook (CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md). Campaigns and agents must read current beliefs + evidence before generate/send. Pair with dossier describe-not-score: per-account timeline with field provenance and explicit conflicts; trust hierarchy actions\u2192voice\u2192CRM; scoring agents consume dossiers and never rewrite them.", "first_slice": "Pick one live client pack. Scaffold context/ + sources/ + releases/ + AGENTS.md rulebook; migrate existing ICP/voice into versioned releases. Add pre-flight gate: agent run fails if it skips reading context/ and searching sources/. Ship one account dossier object (timeline + provenance + conflict records) for 10 accounts; block any scoring prompt from writing into the dossier artifact.", "supporting_emails": ["04", "30", "09"]}, {"id": "U-002", "title": "Universal evidence contracts (quote-or-blank, source packets, visibility labels)", "priority": "P0", "theme": "Evidence, trust & safety gates", "summary": "Every scored account/contact claim in orchestrator pipelines must carry openable evidence. Encode: (1) quote must be literal substring or blank claim; (2) PE/signal source packet = URL + captured passage + unknown fields; (3) visibility labels snapshot-proven | record-dated | assumed \u2014 assumed never enters campaign-ready tells; (4) confirmed-vs-inferred tiering for tech-install and similar lists; (5) dual death columns weak_evidence vs unproven_visibility.", "first_slice": "Add EvidencePacket schema + eval to one buying-signal campaign path: reject rows missing quote/URL/passage; stamp visibility label; quarantine assumed. Wire into client-pack export so unsupported claims cannot enter send queues.", "supporting_emails": ["01", "03", "06", "07", "09", "10", "28"]}, {"id": "U-003", "title": "Research-ladder enrichment (free\u2192cheap\u2192expensive) with verdict-packet bus", "priority": "P0", "theme": "Cost-aware research & enrichment orchestration", "summary": "Replace frontier-by-default enrichment with Crawford-style escalation: free floors (wire/DNS/CT/parked sentinels, Common Crawl/registries, dual-source settle) \u2192 cheap page-to-text/search/small models \u2192 budgeted agent only on stubborn residue. Verdict packets travel with each row as hard constraints higher floors cannot un-know (parked, wrong-jurisdiction twin, aggregator-only). Absence must be earned; mid-confidence rows go to hold/review queue with category spend tradeoffs. Target economics: ~$0.005/row verify class, free-first TAM harvest $0 on HTML path.", "first_slice": "Implement ladder module on one list-verify job (150\u2013500 rows): free wire+parked+dual-source official-website checks; escalate only misses; attach verdict packet; emit cost receipt + hold queue. Blind-sample 50 rows with grade\u22658.0 ship gate before client export.", "supporting_emails": ["09", "18", "08", "31", "16"]}, {"id": "U-004", "title": "Hard-kill \u2192 judge pipeline with suppressed-row audit", "priority": "P0", "theme": "Evidence, trust & safety gates", "summary": "Reusable pre-LLM rule engine before any generative spend: negative lists, geo, employment type, title/body mismatches, pay floors, dead domains, generic mailboxes, deterministic tenure/anti-hopping. Log every kill with killing rule on a Suppressed tab. Only survivors hit batched judges (~20/agent). Classify-only models for bulk ranking; reserve generative LLMs for copy/dossier.", "first_slice": "Extract hard-kill config schema + suppressed audit sheet for one campaign type (job-signal or contact rank). Run: 1k rows \u2192 log kill reasons \u2192 judge only survivors; measure $ saved vs judge-all baseline.", "supporting_emails": ["03", "18", "02", "17"]}, {"id": "U-005", "title": "Spend honesty gates (plain-plan + arithmetic + cost-guard tiers)", "priority": "P0", "theme": "Evidence, trust & safety gates", "summary": "Before any captain-budget or paid-lookup run: require ninth-grade plan brief (deliverable, plain method, cost, warnings, questions, human move). Arithmetic honesty eval rejects unit_cost\u00d7volume\u2260stated_total (catch $50\u201375 headline vs ~$700 line math). Tiered LLM cost-guard: <$100 auto, $100\u2013$1k confirm, >$1k typed amount within $5, >$10k second confirm. Cost ledger stated before spend and read after. Route-split calibrator: sample N domains \u2192 free/JS/Cloudflare/dead % \u2192 forecast full-market finish cost before escalation.", "first_slice": "Add plain-plan schema + arithmetic eval + cost-guard wrapper around one enrichment agent. Force row_count\u00d7unit_price=total preview on paid residue. Block export if plan math disagrees or spend exceeds guard without override reason.", "supporting_emails": ["12", "09", "22", "03", "18"]}, {"id": "U-006", "title": "Outcome-backed ICP archetypes (win-loss-rewind + visibility-proven tells)", "priority": "P0", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "Replace Apollo-style firmographic ICP filters with per-archetype weighted public-signal rubrics from labeled outcomes (won/lost/healthy/churned/expanded). Phase 0 data_confidence gate (CRM vs LinkedIn/site/headcount quarantine). Reconstruct pre-buy public state 6\u201318mo; hypothesis\u2192pain-prove kill\u219220% holdout lift. Ban headcount/industry/revenue as sole discriminators. Win-loss backcast prover: Wayback/Common Crawl dates visibility vs per-deal reference date; only snapshot-proven/record-dated tells enter scoring.", "first_slice": "For one vertical client: Phase 0 data_confidence.md on CRM outcomes; run win-loss-rewind on held-out deals; emit one archetype fit_score rubric with train/holdout lift; wire fit_score into campaign routing for a 200-account pilot. Attach visibility labels via prover on top 20 tells.", "supporting_emails": ["25", "06", "10", "30"]}, {"id": "U-007", "title": "Job-post buying-signal ranker (JoJo pattern) + people handoff", "priority": "P1", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "Embeddings rank frozen+live ATS job corpus (~16 hiring systems) by semantic fit to ICP pain prompt; emit ranked companies with posting quotes only. Freeze+live overlay+liveness sample (% still open). JoJo\u2192Crawford handoff: research agent attaches verified people with confidence and disproves against public record (drop job-changers, ghosts, ended roles). Flip-rank call sheets: re-rank corpus per target company ~15s for outbound.", "first_slice": "Stand up dated job corpus slice + embeddings ranker for one ICP prompt; ship 10-company call sheet with quotes + verified contacts; drop unproven names; report liveness sample on freeze.", "supporting_emails": ["07", "03", "09"]}, {"id": "U-008", "title": "Public-record Blueprint playbook pipeline + source adapters", "priority": "P1", "theme": "Playbooks, channels & campaigns", "summary": "URL\u2192ICP/pain inference\u2192public-record hunt\u2192segment score vs gates\u21922 pain-qualified + 2 permissionless-gift drafts + math worksheet. First-class adapters for OSHA/EPA/HHS breach/FDIC/CFPB/PACER/HUD/CMS/etc. Segment gates: fresh time window, product-aligned failure mode, buyer-clickable proof field. Human rewrite queue; never auto-send. Seed vertical starter packs from published shapes.", "first_slice": "One vertical adapter (e.g. OSHA Establishment Search) + playbook generator for one client URL; emit segment+evidence rows+drafts+worksheet into client pack; gate on proof fields before send path.", "supporting_emails": ["10", "01", "06", "25"]}, {"id": "U-009", "title": "Account title-enumeration \u2192 person-rank cascade", "priority": "P1", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "Enumerate-all-then-filter at large accounts: domain\u2192all owned company pages\u2192full unique-title vocabulary\u2192title score\u2192full-profile person score (5\u21921 taxonomy)\u2192N fixed buyer questions. Buyer-rules page from calls + eight-field person packet only. Multi-page orphan detector blocks 'list complete' while large unqueried pages remain. Company-then-person scoring; competitor-employer boost optional. Cascade via classify-only models (~$0.00012/person class).", "first_slice": "For one enterprise account: discover owned pages, build title vocab, score titles, expand top titles to ranked call list; add orphan-page check; persist buyer-rules + packet schema in client pack.", "supporting_emails": ["05", "02", "24", "17"]}, {"id": "U-010", "title": "TAM contact harvester (free-first) + name\u2194email gates", "priority": "P1", "theme": "List-building & TAM inventory", "summary": "Domain CSV\u2192homepage triage buckets (HTML-email/static/JS/Cloudflare/dead)\u2192deterministic extract (mailto, CF XOR email, JSON-LD, team cards)\u2192person-tied vs generic quarantine\u2192Flash-Lite name enrich on disk corpus\u2192dual name\u2194email gates (literal name-in-text + adversarial reject-default)\u2192budget-capped paid residue only. Route-split cost calibrator from sample before full market.", "first_slice": "Ship harvest.py path on 150\u2013500 domain market: free pass + verify_names; report named-contact rate + person/generic split; hard-cap paid tier at $5; block fabrications via dual gates.", "supporting_emails": ["18", "09", "27"]}, {"id": "U-011", "title": "Multi-source TAM discovery swarm (gov/assoc/Maps/open-data)", "priority": "P1", "theme": "List-building & TAM inventory", "summary": "Phase-gated skill: API-key kill switch\u2192silent client-site read\u2192narrow company-type gate\u2192five parallel lanes (federal registries, state licensing, associations, Maps/local APIs, open data)\u2192fixed scorecard (Coverage 35/Websites 30/Quality 20/Cost 15; gov wins ties)\u2192recommend 2\u20133 source combo\u2192phone dedupe merge. Compelled-license adjacency skill (what licenses must they hold?). Target ~85% TAM at ~$0\u2013$10/run vs aggregator ceiling.", "first_slice": "Run discovery for one narrow vertical; emit scored source table + recommended combo; pull 2 sources, phone-dedupe, drop reachable inventory into campaign queue.", "supporting_emails": ["27", "26", "18", "10"]}, {"id": "U-012", "title": "PDF + pixels-only contact extractors (row-crop OCR)", "priority": "P1", "theme": "List-building & TAM inventory", "summary": "Discover contact-rich PDFs (licensing boards, agency rosters, associations, conference PDFs); triage text vs scanned; text path pypdf+$0; vision path 200DPI + ink-density row crops + parallel OCR waves (not whole-page ~58%). Screen-recording attendee/roster path: same row-crop rule; ban Gemini-class hallucination on this path; sample-verify vs frames; audit coverage \u226597%. Shrink-unit/parallelize/merge generalizes to other batch reads.", "first_slice": "PDF triage+text extract on one known roster PDF\u2192CSV; add row-crop vision path behind flag; coverage audit sample. Separately: one screen-recording attendee pane\u2192row-crop\u2192CSV with frame sample-verify gate.", "supporting_emails": ["26", "32"]}, {"id": "U-013", "title": "People-list verification ladder (live-profile \u2192 re-hunt \u2192 adversarial skeptic)", "priority": "P1", "theme": "Cost-aware research & enrichment orchestration", "summary": "Composable skill for any people census: (1) live-profile claim check via URL-as-data proxy; (2) re-hunt flagged/name-collision rows; (3) strongest-model adversarial audit on action tier allowed to kill unverifiable claims. Enrichment only after LinkedIn confirm. Enrichment-filter known-answer probe (fail closed if Blitz-class APIs silently ignore filters). Exa people-search\u2192enrich\u2192Haiku validate (~39% FP catch); mode A/B so deep-reasoning wins people-at-company vs Deep Search trap.", "first_slice": "Wire three-rung ladder after one people pull; probe enrichment filters on known set; add Exa category:people + Haiku validate; quarantine FPs; report coverage lift and false-positive drop.", "supporting_emails": ["08", "07", "09", "31"]}, {"id": "U-014", "title": "Tech install-base finder (fingerprint-first, confirmed customers only)", "priority": "P1", "theme": "List-building & TAM inventory", "summary": "Product name+domain\u2192fingerprint (DNS tenants, headers, HTML/cookies, auth differentials, CT)\u2192multi-source harvest\u2192scale probe\u2192identity extract\u2192firmographic enrich\u2192confirmed-customer CSV. BuiltWith/G2/jobs/LinkedIn stay inferred candidates only. Binary confirmation artifacts required.", "first_slice": "Fingerprint one competitor/adjacent product; probe harvested candidates; ship confirmed CSV with evidence artifacts; keep inferred bucket separate from send-ready.", "supporting_emails": ["28", "09", "07"]}, {"id": "U-015", "title": "Channel-finder market\u2192rank\u2192act module", "priority": "P1", "theme": "Playbooks, channels & campaigns", "summary": "ICP/market in\u2192concentric buyer picture (world knowledge, market wisdom, buyer-voice from calls)\u2192wide 10\u201350 channel census\u2192compelled/trust/break-fix hunt\u2192page-open proof\u2192code 8-factor scorecard\u2192Monday next-step rows (role+contact+action). Per-channel hunter/checker/verifier swarm with concurrency throttle. Blind cold-pass eval rediscovers compelled channels.", "first_slice": "Run for one client vertical; emit ranked underpriced+unused channels with next steps; require page-open proof; store scorecard in code not LLM opinion.", "supporting_emails": ["11", "14", "04"]}, {"id": "U-016", "title": "PE acquisition / ownership-change signal scout", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Playbooks, channels & campaigns", "summary": "Monthly scout: PE deal leads\u2192normalized transaction groups (acquired, direct buyer, PE backers; platform vs add-on)\u2192deal-status gates (agreement vs completion)\u2192source packets\u2192supported vs unresolved quarantine. Ownership-graph data layer for account routing.", "first_slice": "Schema for transaction groups + status fields; ingest one month of PE announcements into signal queue; quarantine incomplete; gate one post-acquisition campaign on completion status.", "supporting_emails": ["01", "10", "08"]}, {"id": "U-017", "title": "Market KOL influence-graph + Influence\u00d7Independence\u00d7Recruitability", "priority": "P2", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "Regional research agents assemble named influence universe; eight-dimension evidence scoring (not title); multiply Influence\u00d7Independence\u00d7Recruitability; split board shortlist vs competitive-intel (captured by rivals). Cheap triage then deep research only on bar-clearers (~3%).", "first_slice": "One vertical: fan out regional scouts, score subset, emit dual lists with approach angles; wire client competitor list into Independence.", "supporting_emails": ["13", "16"]}, {"id": "U-018", "title": "Referral-notability customer ranking (owner influence, not revenue)", "priority": "P2", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "CRM customer list\u2192owner-resolve (distrust CRM contact)\u2192LinkedIn+footprint\u21920\u2013100 Referral Notability\u2192tiers \u226555 affiliate / \u226580 VIP. Cheap-first cascade; case-safe artifact keys (CRM ID case-collision fix). Dual sort influence vs revenue in packs.", "first_slice": "Score one client's ~200\u20131200 customers; resolve owners; emit \u226555 tier list for referral asks; case-safe evidence keys; side-by-side revenue rank.", "supporting_emails": ["16", "13"]}, {"id": "U-019", "title": "Competitor social/LinkedIn watcher + vision-on-attachments", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Playbooks, channels & campaigns", "summary": "Poll public competitor company updates for launches, events, hiring; vision on poster images for sub-events captions miss (~4\u00d7 schedule). Hypothesis\u2192repeated pattern registry (document failure cases before promote). Leaf\u2192skill wiki wiring for discoverability.", "first_slice": "Watch 3 competitor LinkedIn pages; emit structured signals; run vision on image posts; tag vision/pagination as hypothesis until failure cases written.", "supporting_emails": ["33", "01", "20"]}, {"id": "U-020", "title": "Triple deep-research fan-out + asymmetric-claim synthesis", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Cost-aware research & enrichment orchestration", "summary": "Fan one brief to Claude/ChatGPT/Gemini deep research; corroborate\u2192link skeptic\u2192date skeptic\u2192usefulness judge\u2192synthesize with stable claim IDs. Asymmetric (one-of-three verified) claims lead; consensus is baseline. Evidence-gate: no claim into packs without non-hallucinated cite. Invoke-routing: skip for <5min lookups / account-owned data.", "first_slice": "One client competitive/regulatory brief through multi-engine path (or partial Triple); ship claim table + verification appendix into artifact store; block unverified cites from messaging.", "supporting_emails": ["20", "09", "04"]}, {"id": "U-021", "title": "Honest campaign-influence attribution + call\u00d7search demand engine", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Playbooks, channels & campaigns", "summary": "Salesforce Customizable Campaign Influence sourced vs influenced; engagement_reach never headline; reject deal-create-before-attendance. Dead-rollup diagnostic. Demand engine: transcript swarm clusters buyer problems\u2192search volume\u2192Run now / Own it / Market-led for webinar/content/outbound topic selection.", "first_slice": "Four-tab report for one webinar-heavy client: webinars, topics, what-to-run-next, deals; enforce three-number credit schema; mine calls for next 5 topic verdicts.", "supporting_emails": ["14", "11", "23"]}, {"id": "U-022", "title": "Pre-dossier data auditor (math-first joins/canonical fields)", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Client context, dossier & artifact federation", "summary": "Hard gate before account-intel/dossier agents: domains\u2192joins\u2192canonical revenue\u2192sanity. Python counts match rates to disk; AI only labels severity from numbers. Canonical field + do-not-use registry. Auditor\u2194builder handshake file (use/skip/failures).", "first_slice": "Auditor on one client multi-system folder; write handshake; block dossier build on hard fail unless override-with-reason; register canonical MRR vs zombie revenue columns.", "supporting_emails": ["21", "30", "04"]}, {"id": "U-023", "title": "Editorial Frame + LLM-tell lint + customer-voice rewriter", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Deliverable quality & operator UX", "summary": "Section score 1\u20135 on five editorial questions; default cut; BLUF recommendation first; 60% word cap; twelve hard-hit AI-tell patterns + vocab blocklist with one retry then halt; chart sub-agent Tufte rules; transcript-conditioned voice (Gong/Otter/Fathom/Sybill). Slack-safe lint as delivery boundary for status recaps. Decision-page deliverable shape (recommendation up top, evidence appendix). Demote pure design-taste capture to optional.", "first_slice": "Attach Editorial Frame + lint gate to client-pack brief + campaign status Slack path; freeze quotes/numbers; require override-with-reason on second lint fail.", "supporting_emails": ["23", "29", "15"]}, {"id": "U-024", "title": "Cheap-gate cascade pattern (asset/condition or signal first, enrich last)", "priority": "P3", "theme": "Deliverable quality & operator UX", "summary": "Generalize parking-lot cascade: free geometry/attribute filters\u2192cheap is-the-thing gate\u2192score survivors\u2192paid enrich only on ranked tail. Threshold/noise eval (\u226455 actionable vs <70 flood). Match-claim-to-pixels honesty for physical-world ICPs. Prefer for field-service client packs; not core SaaS default.", "first_slice": "Document cascade template in orchestrator; apply to one non-parking signal class (e.g. job-signal or domain triage) with cutoff eval vs noise band.", "supporting_emails": ["19", "02", "09", "18"]}, {"id": "U-025", "title": "Full-filter company sweep + Sheet/Doc shortlist client pack", "priority": "P3", "theme": "List-building & TAM inventory", "summary": "Enumerate every company matching ICP/headcount/geo via flat-rate search API (no ~50 sample); QPS-aware batching; company-then-person score; competitor boost; survivor deep-scrape; 3-tab Sheet + Doc top-N narratives as permissionless shortlist deliverable. Adjacent to hiring; reuse for outbound universe builds.", "first_slice": "One ICP filter full sweep at reduced geo; company-context before person score; deliver Sheet+Doc pack; compare yield vs sampled-50 baseline.", "supporting_emails": ["24", "17", "05"]}, {"id": "U-026", "title": "Degree-of-fit sales-motion scorer (aligned/partial/not) \u2014 demoted hiring surface", "priority": "P3", "theme": "ICP, scoring & account intelligence", "summary": "Eight sales-context dimensions as people-object schema; score against target motion not prestige; flex-band + open-dimension screen flags. High transfer for contact ranking; shipped surface is AE hiring \u2014 keep as shared scoring schema, not a hiring product in gtm-orchestrator.", "first_slice": "Add eight dimension fields to people schema; score one outbound persona list against client sales motion; report 8-of-8 vs flex.", "supporting_emails": ["17", "02"]}, {"id": "U-027", "title": "Exa/search mode-router + staleness self-heal skill", "priority": "P2", "theme": "Cost-aware research & enrichment orchestration", "summary": "Route deep-reasoning vs deep/fast/instant by job; A/B harness on fixed company set; encode gotchas (category:people, single-item includeText, MCP key-in-URL); on-invoke >30-day staleness refresh from vendor docs. Per-validated-contact cost eval (~$0.04 benchmark).", "first_slice": "Mode-router skill + gotcha config; A/B 20 companies people-at-company; meter $/validated contact; fail raw matches without validation.", "supporting_emails": ["31", "15", "09"]}, {"id": "U-028", "title": "DEPENDS_ON federation + owners/reviews/releases for GTM artifacts", "priority": "P1", "theme": "Client context, dossier & artifact federation", "summary": "ICP, positioning, voice, buyer-language as owned packages with proposed diffs, owner review, cut releases. Campaigns pin release versions. DEPENDS_ON so upstream release flags downstream campaign/agent refresh. Buyer-language\u2192campaign skill from call transcripts with source links.", "first_slice": "Version positioning@vN + voice@vN for one client; pin one campaign to those releases; change positioning and show refresh flag on dependent sequence.", "supporting_emails": ["04", "30", "23"]}], "digests": [{"n": "01", "subject": "Find the companies PE firms just bought", "date": "2026-09-21", "file": "01-find-the-companies-pe-firms-just-bought.md", "mechanism_short": "**PE Acquisition Monitor** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (reusable instructions + scripts) that researches US-connected private-equity acquisitions and emits structured transaction groups. Concrete behavior: - Scopes research month-by-month for (a) US PE firms buying anywhere and (b) US companies bought by foreign PE. - Separates three entity layers per deal: **acquired company**, **direct/operating buyer", "mechanism_md": "**PE Acquisition Monitor** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (reusable instructions + scripts) that researches US-connected private-equity acquisitions and emits structured transaction groups.\n\nConcrete behavior:\n- Scopes research month-by-month for (a) US PE firms buying anywhere and (b) US companies bought by foreign PE.\n- Separates three entity layers per deal: **acquired company**, **direct/operating buyer**, and **PE backers** of that buyer (platform vs add-on). Facts (e.g. headcount) stay on the company whose row they belong to.\n- Splits **agreement** vs **completion** into their own months, then links both announcements to one acquisition so they are not double-counted. Stores event date + what that date means (publication vs expected close vs completed).\n- Ships a **source packet** per record: captured source text, original URL, and supporting passages. Incomplete profiles and unresolved leads stay separate from supported groups.\n- Demo path: give the skill a month, market, and research agent; it builds a new list from collected sources. Original Jan 1\u2013Sep 18, 2026 run produced **634** supported transaction groups (481 participant announcements + 153 industry reporting); market completeness unmeasured.\n\nIllustrative cases in the post: 2X \u2192 The Kiln (Recognize / Insight Partners as PE backers); Clearlake \u2192 Qualus from New Mountain (Mar 2026 agreement, Apr 2026 completion announcement).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-008", "U-016", "U-019"]}, {"n": "02", "subject": "Rank Every Person in a Company for Just $0.00012 Each With Jev", "date": "2026-09-19", "file": "02-rank-every-person-in-a-company-for-just-0-00012.md", "mechanism_short": "A three-stage \u201ctitle sweep \u2192 person score \u2192 buyer questions\u201d pipeline that ranks everyone an index lists at a large account for a salesperson\u2019s product (city-council / permit tracking sold into a ~100k-employee US cable operator). 1. **Title read (prior post / Title Sweep):** Score every distinct job title at the company. August roster: 79,601 people, 23,369 titles; Jev scored all titles for $0.37", "mechanism_md": "A three-stage \u201ctitle sweep \u2192 person score \u2192 buyer questions\u201d pipeline that ranks everyone an index lists at a large account for a salesperson\u2019s product (city-council / permit tracking sold into a ~100k-employee US cable operator).\n\n1. **Title read (prior post / Title Sweep):** Score every distinct job title at the company. August roster: 79,601 people, 23,369 titles; Jev scored all titles for $0.37 in 53 seconds; 315 people landed in the top title score. Turned the rep\u2019s title-search list of 60 (one department, 10 with phones) into 467 callables.\n\n2. **Person read (this post):** Pull full profiles for everyone the index has a full profile for (44,637 of 79,601) and score each person 5\u21921 for the product. Full-profile scoring cost $5.61; end-to-end for all 79,601 people = $9.47 \u2248 **$0.00012 / person**.\n\n3. **Buyer-today read:** For people who could plausibly buy, ask **five fixed questions** per person; output is the list the rep calls. Paid tier claims 1,084 people accepted by those five questions and still at the company (exact question text / lists behind paywall).\n\n**Jev (TypeSafe AI, via OpenRouter):** Classify-only model \u2014 hand it text + a question with a fixed answer list; it picks one answer and a confidence. Cannot write a sentence. That is why bulk reads are cheap: input **$0.042 per million words**, answer cost nothing.\n\n**Score meanings (product-specific):** 5 = buyer / owning dept (gov & franchise affairs, market expansion & buildout, competitive & market intel \u2014 company said competitor watching was the buy reason); 4 = daily users who execute what 5s decide; 3 = adjacent dept; 2 \u2248 nine titles in ten; 1 = exclude (here: finance).\n\n**Packet (eight fields + buyer rules):** roster title; profile headline; full About; up to three current roles (title/company/dates + is-at-target-company flag); two most recent past roles; location; still-at-company (settled in code before Jev); packet fullness flag. Plus a written page of **buyer rules from the account\u2019s own calls**. No name, photo, or company description in the packet. Prefer dated current-role entries over stored roster titles when hand-checking.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-004", "U-009", "U-024", "U-026"]}, {"n": "03", "subject": "03-i-built-a-job-search-agent-live-it-cost-1", "date": "2026-09-04", "file": "03-i-built-a-job-search-agent-live-it-cost-1.md", "mechanism_short": "Claude Code skill **Job Hunter** (live build; Edge Copilot annual install). For AE \u201cDana\u201d (pseudonym): 1. **Sweep** market postings in the market\u2019s own title vocabulary (not candidate jargon); one extra read per company in her relationship graph for that employer\u2019s postings only. 2. **Nine hard kills in code** before any model: negative list, wrong country, wrong employment type, \u201cremote\u201d in title", "mechanism_md": "Claude Code skill **Job Hunter** (live build; Edge Copilot annual install). For AE \u201cDana\u201d (pseudonym):\n\n1. **Sweep** market postings in the market\u2019s own title vocabulary (not candidate jargon); one extra read per company in her relationship graph for that employer\u2019s postings only.\n2. **Nine hard kills in code** before any model: negative list, wrong country, wrong employment type, \u201cremote\u201d in title vs office in body, pay >20% below floor, plus four more. Survivors checked against employer careers boards (Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workable, SmartRecruiters) \u2192 live / closed / unverified + check date.\n3. **Model judge** only on survivors (~20 postings per Sonnet agent): fit score, plain fit reason, reason it might not, one quoted sentence from the posting.\n4. **Lane B (hidden market):** former clients/vendors, colleagues\u2019 employers, competitors of former employers, companies posting adjacent roles with no closer, public \u201cwe\u2019re hiring.\u201d Each door: warmth score, angle, role to approach, draft note. Named people only after she opts in per run. Tool never sends.\n5. **Four separate experience clocks:** sales, industry, systems, total (so \u201c5+ years SaaS sales\u201d scores against sales years, not total tenure). Pay judged on OTE; omitted pay flagged and kept. Ghost-job score from structural signals only.\n6. **Outputs (private to her; scrubbed public copies linked):** 3-page Google Doc brief (funnel counts, 25 apply with one pre-send question each, 25 email companies + warmth, week cost) via Google Doc Builder + second editor that rejected story skeleton twice; 6-tab Sheet \u2014 Apply, Warm doors, Watch (168 past cap), Changes, Suppressed (all 239 + killing rule), Run log (counts + dollars).\n7. **Contracts:** every quote verified as real substring in code (fail \u2192 blank, never paraphrase); every row including #1 carries a \u201cmight not\u201d reason; cost stated before spend and read from ledger after; \u201cover-qualified\u201d only as years band in posting\u2019s words; her data never ships \u2014 scrub gate refused bundle twice (e.g. partner name in a comment) and deletes staged bundle on refusal.\n8. **V1 run counts:** 1,029 read \u2192 239 hard-killed \u2192 196 already closed \u2192 374 judged \u2192 25 apply + 25 warm-door companies. 18/25 apply rows unverified (v1 still showed as apply). Run cost **$1.41**.\n9. **V2 (post-recording, GPT-6 Astra / Codex; not yet shipped to subscribers):** warmth needs dated growth/change event + dated careers check + mandate with serious case against; unverified out of Apply (hold in Watch); packet before any ask (company context, named public people, evidence for/against, permissionless value prop, \u226490-min work sample on public/synthetic inputs, drafted outreach + follow-ups \u2014 nothing sent); profile claims link to resume/sample/interview receipts; total cap 25/50/\u2026/200 (default ~\u00bd open, ~\u2153 hidden, rest relationships); Sheet edits/status survive re-run; four new tabs (packets, people, value props, action state); Google publish creates empty file, strips sharing, verifies, then uploads (simulated only so far). Local suite: 100 tests; before/after on 3 frozen fictional cases: 14\u219220/21 behavioral, 6\u219214/14 acceptance. V2 cost unmeasured.\n\nReal-profile bugs fixed before ship: Unicode line separator splitting records; dual fingerprint scripts marking every row changed; single-word industry false positives (\u201cwellness\u201d in benefits, \u201carchitect\u201d as verb) \u2014 tier now needs two distinct keywords or title match; closed-never-judged no longer route as side doors; separate caps for postings vs warm doors; re-run updates same Sheet.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-004", "U-005", "U-007"]}, {"n": "04", "subject": "04-your-context-is-the-bottleneck-not-your-model", "date": "2026-08-16", "file": "04-your-context-is-the-bottleneck-not-your-model.md", "mechanism_short": "Jordan did not ship a new tool in this issue. He hosted Jacob Dietle\u2019s **Context OS** (filesystem \u201cLLM wiki\u201d) pattern for GTM teams and added three short notes. **Mechanism (Jacob\u2019s, as described):** 1. **Thesis:** Agent quality is hard-capped by context quality. Single-player context = quality of what you ship; multiplayer context = whether org learnings compound or stay trapped (stale ICP copies", "mechanism_md": "Jordan did not ship a new tool in this issue. He hosted Jacob Dietle\u2019s **Context OS** (filesystem \u201cLLM wiki\u201d) pattern for GTM teams and added three short notes.\n\n**Mechanism (Jacob\u2019s, as described):**\n1. **Thesis:** Agent quality is hard-capped by context quality. Single-player context = quality of what you ship; multiplayer context = whether org learnings compound or stay trapped (stale ICP copies, buyer language stuck in calls, product decisions without reusable evidence/reasoning).\n2. **Back-to-files:** Slack/HubSpot/Notion/Salesforce hide files behind UIs/DBs. With Claude Code / Codex / opencode, AI navigates the filesystem \u2014 so Context OS is \u201call files in a filesystem,\u201d not a new app stack.\n3. **Minimal personal Context OS layout:**\n   - `context-os/README.md` (human map)\n   - `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` (agent operating instructions \u2014 not human docs)\n   - `context/` \u2014 current beliefs: `about.md`, `audience.md`, `positioning.md`, `product.md`, `voice.md`\n   - `sources/` \u2014 raw notes, transcripts, evidence\n   - `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` \u2014 repeatable methods\n   - `work/` \u2014 drafts; `releases/` \u2014 approved reusable outputs\n4. **Roles:** sources = what happened; context = what you currently believe; skills = how to do repeatable work; work = intermediates; releases = approved reuse. Start with one directory + one rulebook + repeatedly needed context \u2014 capture first, classify later.\n5. **Rulebook (drop into CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md):** before work \u2014 identify outcome, read `/context`, search `/sources` and `/work`, read relevant `SKILL.md`; working rules \u2014 never silently rewrite evidence, separate verified vs inferred, preserve author language / mark agent additions, prefer update over `final_v2`, only move to `/releases` on approval, leave system clearer.\n6. **Example skill:** `skills/guest-post/SKILL.md` \u2014 inventory sources \u2192 one-sentence argument \u2192 outline \u2192 trace claims \u2192 assemble section-by-section \u2192 preserve language \u2192 check repetition/abstraction/unsupported claims; definition of done includes author review/accept.\n7. **Multiplayer layer:** owners, reviews, releases (four steps) so collaboration isn\u2019t mediated by one bottleneck; Git enforces proposed changes as reviewable snapshots (content-addressable lineage) instead of silent overwrite or `positioning_v2.md` copies.\n8. **Knowledge dependencies / federation:** packages declare `OWNER`, `RELEASE` version, and `DEPENDS_ON: package@version` so upstream changes flag downstream work; same rulebook, distributed ownership; Git can sit under a friendlier UI so non-Git users still get owner/review/history/version.\n9. **Structural influence outcomes claimed:** CS \u2192 engineering PR via permissioned Claude + engineering skills (pattern + quotes + call links as full spec); partnerships see persona change as timelapse of belief/test/learning; marketing ranks/releases buyer phrases from sales calls; sales/product/engineering subscribe to tone-of-voice releases.\n\nJordan\u2019s notes only: (1) non-engineers can run terminal Context OS \u2014 he logged **810 prompts in 17 days** figuring out Claude Code; (2) build the 80% verifiable/iterable categorization, not perfect ontology (cites Clay Shirky 2005 via Jacob); (3) Jacob\u2019s system is the machine that produces GTM \u201cground truth\u201d (ties to Jordan\u2019s prior posts on ground truth \u2192 action and thin AI content).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-001", "U-015", "U-020", "U-022", "U-028"]}, {"n": "05", "subject": "Stop Guessing Job Titles, Find Every Job Title and Filter Down", "date": "2026-08-09", "file": "05-stop-guessing-job-titles-find-every-job-title-and-filter.md", "mechanism_short": "**Enumerate-first, then filter** \u2014 invert title-guess search at a large account: pull every reachable person across every company page the account owns, collect the company\u2019s real job-title vocabulary, score those titles, and filter down to a scored roster and ranked call lists. Rebuild outline (free text): **domain in \u2192 scored roster + ranked call lists out**. Full rebuild recipe and trap math si", "mechanism_md": "**Enumerate-first, then filter** \u2014 invert title-guess search at a large account: pull every reachable person across every company page the account owns, collect the company\u2019s real job-title vocabulary, score those titles, and filter down to a scored roster and ranked call lists. Rebuild outline (free text): **domain in \u2192 scored roster + ranked call lists out**. Full rebuild recipe and trap math sit behind the paywall; annual subscribers install the shipped tool with one command. Geeky path: copy into Claude Code and rebuild.\n\n**Concrete case (as stated):** Rep selling a product that tracks city councils and permits into a ~100k-employee cable operator. Careful title-first build (government affairs, network deployment, site acquisition) returned **60** people, **10** with phones, all in one function \u2014 the buyer was not on the list. Title-first search is a closed loop: it only returns titles you already imagined; titles you didn\u2019t imagine leave no trace of their absence.\n\n**Enumeration flip:** Every LinkedIn page the operator owns \u2192 every person on those pages \u2192 **79,601** reachable people across **14** pages, **23,369** distinct job titles. One of those pages held **26,259** people and had never been queried. Gap (60 vs 79,601) framed as vocabulary problem (seller supplies the titles), not a data-quality failure.\n\n**Same move as prior posts (linked in email):** scoring 15,489 account executives to find 135; turning a million job posts into buying signals \u2014 pointed at a single account: enumerate first, then let the data tell you what to look for.\n\n**Paywalled below-the-line topics (named in free email; details not in free body):**\n1. Four traps that make a big-account pull quietly wrong (each with measured numbers)\n2. Call-notes step that surfaced **58** people in a function no one was calling\n3. Full rebuild recipe (domain \u2192 scored roster / ranked call lists)\n4. What a **95%** coverage figure actually measures, and the name it missed", "upgrade_ids": ["U-009", "U-025"]}, {"n": "06", "subject": "Find your next best customer by knowing why your last best customer chose you", "date": "2026-07-24", "file": "06-find-your-next-best-customer-by-knowing-why-your-last.md", "mechanism_short": "Win-loss backcasting discipline that refuses to treat today\u2019s web as proof about a past deal. A \u201cprover\u201d tool (Claude Code research agents + Internet Archive Wayback Machine) pins when a public signal was actually visible relative to each deal\u2019s reference date\u2014not the close date stamped on every deal. Every past-claim gets one of three labels; only the first two may count: 1. **Snapshot-proven** \u2014", "mechanism_md": "Win-loss backcasting discipline that refuses to treat today\u2019s web as proof about a past deal. A \u201cprover\u201d tool (Claude Code research agents + Internet Archive Wayback Machine) pins when a public signal was actually visible relative to each deal\u2019s reference date\u2014not the close date stamped on every deal.\n\nEvery past-claim gets one of three labels; only the first two may count:\n1. **Snapshot-proven** \u2014 archive has a dated copy from before the deal; signal is in it (clickable URL + date).\n2. **Record-dated** \u2014 signal from a dated record (permit, filing, press release), with the date haircut to when the record actually surfaced in public (\u201cknowable-by\u201d), not the date printed on the record.\n3. **Assumed** \u2014 only today\u2019s web was seen; allowed in the file as \u201cwe think,\u201d never in the bet-on tells list.\n\nTight visibility windows come from the archive\u2019s distinct-version index (identical re-crawls collapsed) plus a binary search over ~a dozen page reads (demo: \u201cClaude\u201d on anthropic.com absent Mar 11 2023 / present Mar 13 2023 vs Jun 1 2023 cutoff; HubSpot \u201cSOC 2\u201d first present Oct 25 2015). Order of operations flipped: per customer-type scout maps real public sources (license boards, state rosters, permit portals, regulator feeds), confirms each with one live record, and dates archive coverage before any signal brainstorm. Findings that lack dated proof die labeled \u201ccan\u2019t prove\u201d; survivors still must separate wins vs losses on held-out deals. Two death columns kept separate: weak evidence vs couldn\u2019t prove visibility. LinkedIn/login-walled pages: presence can be proven if a snapshot exists; absence never can. Shared rate brake across agents on one machine: 3s request spacing; on refusal all back off together (60s \u2192 2 min \u2192 15 min cap); already-fetched pages cached to disk. First shipped version had a field-name bug that silently returned \u201cunknown\u201d on every backcast check; fixed in the week-of-ship update, plus per-deal reference dates. Common Crawl is the fallback when Wayback has a gap. Annual-subscriber win-loss tool picked up this time-travel layer on Jul 18.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-006", "U-008"]}, {"n": "07", "subject": "07-turn-a-million-job-posts-into-buying-signals", "date": "2026-07-20", "file": "07-turn-a-million-job-posts-into-buying-signals.md", "mechanism_short": "**JoJo** \u2014 small Claude Code program that treats job postings as buying signals (company naming pain, tools, and work it can\u2019t yet do, in its own words). 1. **Corpus:** ~947,456 open postings from public sources across **16 hiring systems** (Greenhouse, Ashby, and peers). Public-domain job data; corpus **frozen at June 25, 2026** (source stopped updating). Separate live feed overlays fresh posting", "mechanism_md": "**JoJo** \u2014 small Claude Code program that treats job postings as buying signals (company naming pain, tools, and work it can\u2019t yet do, in its own words).\n\n1. **Corpus:** ~947,456 open postings from public sources across **16 hiring systems** (Greenhouse, Ashby, and peers). Public-domain job data; corpus **frozen at June 25, 2026** (source stopped updating). Separate live feed overlays fresh postings.\n2. **Read, don\u2019t keyword-match:** Commercial tools flag \u201cis hiring\u201d; JoJo reads the description, scores how directly it describes the problem you sell against, and **keeps a short quote from the posting as evidence**. **No quote \u2192 no claim.**\n3. **Embeddings:** standard cheap technique \u2014 group/score by meaning, not word match \u2014 so scoring ~a million postings costs almost nothing.\n4. **Demo run:** single prompt \u2014 who is investing hardest right now in go-to-market engineering? Ranked every company in the corpus by how directly its postings describe that work. Top names weren\u2019t inferred; they said it (e.g. AvePoint RevOps: \u201cGo-To-Market execution as an engineered system\u201d; Cresta GTM Engineer: \u201cAI native operating system for our revenue engine\u201d; OpenAI GTM Growth Engineering posting). **Ten companies** made the list \u2014 each with a job literally titled for GTM engineering, open at freeze, plus a proving line.\n5. **People half = Crawford:** JoJo reads postings; **Crawford** (separate web-research agent) verifies humans / attaches names. Pair then flips per company: who should *this* company sell to? JoJo **re-ranks the whole corpus ~15 seconds per company**; Crawford attaches person + reason. Across ten sheets: **79 contacts**, each with a confidence tag. Sheets give targeting + proof; a person still writes the note.\n6. **Disprove-before-ship:** Crawford tries to disprove each name against the person\u2019s public record. This run **dropped ten** (job-changers in the prior six weeks; VP Eng whose profile showed ended 2024 / \u201cCyclist\u2026 Retired\u201d while four real VPs exist today; one **ghost card** \u2014 website name with dead LinkedIn, absent from all 67 verified employees \u2192 replaced with marketing director). Unproven names deleted.\n7. **Liveness check on freeze:** sampled **643** frozen postings live off the boards \u2192 **~88%** of checkable ones still open.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-007", "U-013", "U-014"]}, {"n": "08", "subject": "The Second-Time Founder List VCs Can't Buy \u2014 So I Built It", "date": "2026-07-10", "file": "08-the-second-time-founder-list-vcs-can-t-buy-so.md", "mechanism_short": "Pipeline that takes an acquirer list (one brand or fifty) and returns second-time founders from those deal histories: people who sold a startup, finished vesting, and are quietly building again \u2014 ranked 0\u2013100 on how badly a pre-seed investor should want the meeting. Proven at two scales: - Six acquirers at once (Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, Anthropic): 806 acquisitions mined \u2192 736 peop", "mechanism_md": "Pipeline that takes an acquirer list (one brand or fifty) and returns second-time founders from those deal histories: people who sold a startup, finished vesting, and are quietly building again \u2014 ranked 0\u2013100 on how badly a pre-seed investor should want the meeting.\n\nProven at two scales:\n- Six acquirers at once (Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, Anthropic): 806 acquisitions mined \u2192 736 people ranked and contact-enriched.\n- Datadog July 2026 checkable demo: 19 acquisitions in \u2192 13 live-verified founders out, under $1 metered spend.\n\nCore stages (as described):\n1. **Census from deal history** \u2014 Wikipedia acquisition list pages as the starting census; blank domains resolved via Jordan\u2019s free-first domain resolver (never guessed). Founders discovered via web-research agents on acquisition-era TechCrunch/CNBC coverage, Crunchbase/RocketReach snippets, and old team pages via the Wayback Machine \u2014 not via enrichment APIs filtering on past employers (Blitz silently ignores unsupported filters and returns confident-looking junk).\n2. **Live-profile verification** \u2014 every candidate\u2019s LinkedIn fetched through a proxy that takes the URL as data (no browser, no logged-in session); cheap model checks claims against the live headline. Disagreements between press trail and live profile are kept as signal, not flattened; statuses carry confidence grades.\n3. **Re-hunt flagged people** \u2014 second agent wave with tighter identity requirements; corrects name collisions (e.g. same-named healthcare worker; two different \u201cDan Roth\u201d founders on the six-acquirer run).\n4. **Adversarial skeptic pass** \u2014 strongest model audits the top tier (building-but-unfunded) with fresh searches; allowed to kill unverifiable claims.\n5. **Contact enrichment last** \u2014 Blitz only after the right LinkedIn profile is confirmed (emails/phones).\n6. **Vesting-math ranking** \u2014 deal year + ~4 years \u2248 lockup open; scores peak for windows opened in the last two years, ramp for windows opening within one year, fade for long-freed people already committed elsewhere. Serial founders collapsed to a single row with full deal history. Product startups ranked above services/investing/advising; people still at the acquirer (per four-year vesting math) are auto-deprioritized.\n\nOn Datadog the roster split into bullseye (building, no institutional round found), already restarted+funded, sidelined (investing/advising/services), and still-at-acquirer. Six-acquirer run: LinkedIn coverage 40% \u2192 94%; false positives dropped by the verification ladder. Email notes a \u201cgeeky version\u201d / Claude Code recipe for annual subscribers but that recipe body is not in the ingested message \u2014 not reconstructed here.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-003", "U-013", "U-016"]}, {"n": "09", "subject": "09-introducing-crawford-a-claude-code-ai-agent-for-cheap-right", "date": "2026-07-06", "file": "09-introducing-crawford-a-claude-code-ai-agent-for-cheap-right.md", "mechanism_short": "**Crawford** \u2014 a Claude Code research agent optimized for right answers per dollar (not raw accuracy alone). Rows ride a ladder of floors and exit at the first floor that settles them; free floors first, then cheap metered, then an expensive per-row Claude agent only for stubborn residue. **Free floors (catalog + wire + page + cert + quotas)** - **Already-scraped web:** Common Crawl (recent real c", "mechanism_md": "**Crawford** \u2014 a Claude Code research agent optimized for right answers per dollar (not raw accuracy alone). Rows ride a ladder of floors and exit at the first floor that settles them; free floors first, then cheap metered, then an expensive per-row Claude agent only for stubborn residue.\n\n**Free floors (catalog + wire + page + cert + quotas)**\n- **Already-scraped web:** Common Crawl (recent real content?), Overture Maps (~69M places), Foursquare (~108.7M places) with declared website/phone, Chrome public usage (~18.4M sites), domain zone files, plus government registries (fire-department rosters, medical licenses, SEC, nonprofit returns). Crawford\u2019s catalog: **316 free sources**, each tested before slotting.\n- **Wire checks** (thousands of rows/min on a laptop; clear ~10\u201325% of a typical job): dead DNS, parking-company nameservers, missing mail setup.\n- **Skeptical page read:** doesn\u2019t trust HTTP 200 \u2014 parked pages still load. Sentinel phrases (\u201cthis domain is for sale,\u201d ad-frame scaffolding) + garbage-page trick (real site 404s; empty shell serves the same husk).\n- **Certificate heartbeat (CT logs):** first cert = birthday; renewals = pulse; stop + move to parking NS = dated death. Crawford was the only tool in the trap test running this check.\n- **Free quotas with dual-source settle:** Google Knowledge Graph (100k free lookups/day) + Wikidata official-website claims. Same answer from two unrelated sources \u2192 settle free; disagreement or miss \u2192 escalate, never guess.\n- **Absence must be earned:** before \u201cno website,\u201d brute-force ~30 batch-learned naming-convention guesses + one ~half-cent search. Silence is never evidence.\n\n**Cheap floors**\n- Spend existing Clay credits first (when utilization leaves headroom), only where Clay wins (verified-email / mobile waterfalls that bill on delivery).\n- Metered pennies: page-to-text (hundredths of a cent/page); one structured search (~half a cent) per stuck row; small fast model reads fetched pages (~$0.25 / 1k rows).\n- Search answers still pass Crawford identity checks + free fetch of the answered page (bounced wrong-state twin + two county sites the search was confident about). Half-cent search floor settled **22 of 39** scored trap rows with **zero wrong answers**.\n\n**Expensive floor**\n- One Claude agent per stubborn row only; budget 3\u201315 tool calls by difficulty; hard stop when three sources agree; forced best-answer-with-confidence when budget ends.\n- Row arrives as a **packet** carrying cheaper-floor verdicts as constraints the agent can\u2019t un-know (scar from Hudlow miss: agent re-derived without \u201cdomain parked\u201d and shipped the parking page).\n- Closing 12 stubborn rows: straight-to-agent **$0.50** vs designed order (cheap search first) **$0.06** \u2014 labeled in-sample demo only.\n\n**How it can be sure**\n- Three confidence bands: nearly certain \u2192 commit with evidence+cost; nearly certain absence \u2192 commit \u201cnot found\u201d; middle \u2192 hold, never guess; unresolved after top floor \u2192 human review queue.\n- Four truth gates: (1) two sources with separate origins (aggregators never corroborate); (2) identity proven to this org (name + place + jurisdiction); (3) only positive evidence settles; (4) awkward honest verdicts allowed (\u201ctheir domain, no usable website\u201d).\n- Review queue asks category tradeoffs (\u201c~$2 closes ~40 held rows?\u201d), not one-row fixes.\n\n**Self-test harness**\n- Blind agents build a fresh answer key per job (100\u2013200 row sample: half random, quarter suspicious, quarter regional/size spread); two unrelated primary sources required to enter the key.\n- Grade out of 10: accuracy+completeness half; evidence quality + output cleanliness most of rest; cost-efficiency + consistency close. Below **8.0** doesn\u2019t ship. Grade always printed next to measured $/row.\n- Misses clustered by cause \u2192 one plan fix \u2192 re-run slice \u2192 re-score (four of five first-run wrongs were \u201cno website\u201d without discovery; absence-must-be-earned retired them).\n\n**Receipts (as stated)**\n- July 3 proving run (two 150-row client slices): **8.37/10**, **$0.00** marginal (paid floors off); caught 6 parked/dead sites client listed live; ~half of one job held.\n- July 4 after fixes, full ladder: **9.29/10**, **34 of 39** hand-verified correct, **$0.77** total for the 150-row job (~half a cent/row). All 34 committed answers survived the key; five misses were held \u201cunresolved\u201d where truth was \u201cno website\u201d \u2014 hesitating on absence, never shipping wrong.\n- Trap (39 NC fire departments, key locked before any tool): parked pages fooled OpenAI, Claude, Exa; Montana twin (Creston VFD Kalispell) fooled Claude + Parallel\u2019s two cheapest tiers; Claygent clean but hit 25/day test-run cap. Crawford itself had two false-alives pre-fix (county directory committed too late in sequence; Hudlow packet missing parked verdict) \u2014 both became design rules. Ranking not crowned: circular partner pick, free floors vs list accounting, n=39 too small.\n\n**Blueprint Bench (in build)**\n- Per-dollar GTM web-research benchmark: ~1k fresh questions/quarter, 10 types, live from public registries; paid dual human panels; house plays visitor rules (key fingerprint public before run; Crawford config frozen before questions drawn). Headline metric: **Total Cost to Clear** (spend + price on wrongs + cost to close helds; subscription compute at list). Harness: 123 automated tests pass; 54-row pilot run vs three cheap tools ($1.08 contestant spend); trap machine already catches expired\u2192parked as live. First full edition next (~$400\u2013580 to produce). Annual subscribers can install Crawford today.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-001", "U-002", "U-003", "U-005", "U-007", "U-010", "U-013", "U-014", "U-020", "U-024", "U-027"]}, {"n": "10", "subject": "10-programmatic-gtm-playbooks-from-a-clay-advisor-since-2020", "date": "2026-07-01", "file": "10-programmatic-gtm-playbooks-from-a-clay-advisor-since-2020.md", "mechanism_short": "**Blueprint Playbook / programmatic GTM playbook engine** \u2014 hand it one company website; it returns a full GTM playbook (who to target, public records that prove pain, draft messages, and the math behind every number) in about twelve to fifteen minutes. Concrete pass (as described): 1. **Read the seller\u2019s site** \u2014 infer what they sell and who feels the pain. 2. **Hunt public records** that prove a", "mechanism_md": "**Blueprint Playbook / programmatic GTM playbook engine** \u2014 hand it one company website; it returns a full GTM playbook (who to target, public records that prove pain, draft messages, and the math behind every number) in about twelve to fifteen minutes.\n\nConcrete pass (as described):\n1. **Read the seller\u2019s site** \u2014 infer what they sell and who feels the pain.\n2. **Hunt public records** that prove a prospect is in trouble *right now* (illustrative: safety-compliance software \u2192 OSHA enforcement \u2014 inspection number, date, violation type, dollar penalty).\n3. **Build a list-seller-resistant segment** (illustrative: plants cited for willful or repeat OSHA violations in the last ninety days \u2014 the exact failure mode the product prevents).\n4. **Draft outreach** that names the inspection, date, and penalty and hands the reader something useful without asking for anything back.\n5. **Show the math** behind every number used \u2014 buyer can click the underlying record.\n\nThesis: average AI email is free and ignored; edge is situational + competitive context from public records the buyer can\u2019t see aggregated for themselves. Same move every industry: a public record nobody else bothered to read \u2192 reason to call.\n\nReal public playbooks already shipped (examples named; each grounded in a checkable dataset):\n- **Fiserv** \u2014 FDIC enforcement, CFPB complaints, FFIEC deposit data (ZIP-level underservice gaps)\n- **Everlaw** \u2014 PACER federal court records (privilege-log gaps)\n- **BeyondTrust** \u2014 HHS breach portal (healthcare facilities post\u2013insider breach)\n- **Vantage Risk** \u2014 EPA violations + OSHA serious-to-willful reclassification deadlines (oil & gas)\n- **ArrowStream** \u2014 130,000+ restaurant POs + state health inspections (distributor pricing gaps)\n- **DoorLoop** \u2014 HUD inspection scores + expiring housing contracts (failing affordable housing)\n- **Deenova** \u2014 CMS infection metrics + state sterilization citations (hospital instrument tracking)\n- **ComplyAuto** \u2014 OSHA Establishment Search + FTC Safeguards breach data (dealerships nearing willful)\n- **AGRIVI** \u2014 FDA Food Traceability List + USDA data (farms vs FSMA deadlines)\n- **CHA Consulting** \u2014 FTA grants + EPA water-permit data (transit/water utilities with budget gaps)\n\nEngine behavior at scale: scans public records across **fifty-plus sources**, scores segments against the same internal gates Jordan\u2019s team used when building plays by hand in Clay (advisor since 2020), drafts **two pain-qualified** and **two permissionless-gift** messages \u2014 each with worksheet math. Output is deliberately plausible but drafts are starting points: targeting/record/proof are the win; copy still needs a human who lives in that pain to rewrite the last mile.\n\nGenerate at playbooks.blueprintgtm.com (or browse by industry).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-006", "U-008", "U-011", "U-016"]}, {"n": "11", "subject": "11-i-built-a-cheap-channel-finder-for-any-market", "date": "2026-06-28", "file": "11-i-built-a-cheap-channel-finder-for-any-market.md", "mechanism_short": "Tool that takes a market (lawyers, home services, dentists, etc.) and returns a ranked channel list: specific places those buyers pay attention, cost per channel, why it's the bet, and a cheap test plan. Starts from buyer + past channel experiments / market exploration \u2014 not from a fixed menu of ~12 channels. **Concentric-circle buyer picture (three views that argue with each other):** 1. **Innerm", "mechanism_md": "Tool that takes a market (lawyers, home services, dentists, etc.) and returns a ranked channel list: specific places those buyers pay attention, cost per channel, why it's the bet, and a cheap test plan. Starts from buyer + past channel experiments / market exploration \u2014 not from a fixed menu of ~12 channels.\n\n**Concentric-circle buyer picture (three views that argue with each other):**\n1. **Innermost \u2014 world knowledge:** learn the buyer first (e.g. lawyers: where they gather, associations, license-maintenance obligations).\n2. **Outward \u2014 market wisdom:** case studies and marketers already targeting that buyer; competitor congregation (e.g. same conference sponsorship) as signal.\n3. **Buyer voice:** work backwards from what real buyers said \u2014 where they first heard about a vendor, their words; Jordan cites mining recorded sales calls as high-signal and underused.\n\n**Compelled-channel principle:** for any buyer, where are they legally or ethically required to show up? (Lawyers: CLE credits, state bar dues, filing systems they can't opt out of.) Those rooms are framed as underpriced, high-trust alpha most channel checklists miss.\n\n**Run order \u2014 wide then deep:**\n1. Breadth: evaluate ~10\u201350 always-on candidates (paid search, Meta, LinkedIn, Reddit, email, partnerships, associations, podcasts, newsletters, conferences, etc.) without judging yet.\n2. Smarter second pass: hunt channels the checklist would never name \u2014 driven by buyer-specific questions (compelled places, who they trust, where they look when something breaks).\n3. Depth: pressure-test contenders for real audience size, cost, contact; cut the long tail.\n\n**Under the hood:**\n- Swarm of Claude agents: one group studies how others solve channel discovery (before looking at Jordan's own tools, to avoid anchoring); another argues how to build the buyer picture; one agent per channel hunting hard proof that buyers gather/trust there; a second set checks that work; a verifier signs off; only conclusions return to the main agent.\n- Rank is **code over a fixed eight-item scorecard**, not model opinion \u2014 same evidence in \u2192 same number out. Scorecard dimensions named include: how tightly the buyer clusters there, whether real buyers say that's how they found you, whether customers from that channel stick, how contested it already is.\n- Links are not trusted until an agent opens the page (real group + member count, real sponsor page \u2014 not a plausible homepage).\n\n**Blind rediscovery test:** bake principles (\"where compelled,\" \"who do they trust\"), not lawyer answers. Cold pass (buyer profile stripped of fed answers) still named state bar, mandatory CLE, practice-management software lawyers live in, and peer talk groups. Full run scored **330 channels**; **31** ranked genuinely underpriced; **27** openings the business wasn't using. Failures fixed before ship: throttle heavy checkers (rate limits); require page-open proof (wrong Facebook group; subreddit that resolved to a dating board); every channel must include actual role, real contact, and a Monday-morning next step \u2014 ranking alone is not a deliverable. Lawyers = guinea pig; next step is a non-law market to validate.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-015", "U-021"]}, {"n": "12", "subject": "Can you trust what Claude Code is about to do?", "date": "2026-06-25", "file": "12-can-you-trust-what-claude-code-is-about-to-do.md", "mechanism_short": "**Plain Plan Mode** \u2014 a Claude Code plan style that rewrites the pre-run approval surface from an engineer \u201cparts catalog\u201d (file names, settings, command lines) into a newspaper-style brief a non-engineer can actually sign. Four rules that make it work (as stated): 1. **Plain English, ninth-grade.** Short sentences. If a 12-year-old couldn\u2019t read it aloud and know exactly what they\u2019re approving, r", "mechanism_md": "**Plain Plan Mode** \u2014 a Claude Code plan style that rewrites the pre-run approval surface from an engineer \u201cparts catalog\u201d (file names, settings, command lines) into a newspaper-style brief a non-engineer can actually sign.\n\nFour rules that make it work (as stated):\n1. **Plain English, ninth-grade.** Short sentences. If a 12-year-old couldn\u2019t read it aloud and know exactly what they\u2019re approving, rewrite until they can.\n2. **A real \u201chow,\u201d in plain words.** Still names the method and which tools are wired together \u2014 but labels tools the way a newspaper would (e.g. \u201ca web-research engine built for AI\u201d), not insider code names. Approver signs the method, not a mystery.\n3. **Five little symbols, each meaning one thing.** What you get / what it costs / a warning / a question for you / your move \u2014 so the eye routes itself in one read.\n4. **Honesty rule on every number.** If two numbers in the plan multiply into a third, they must agree (per-item price \u00d7 count = total). Headline cost and line items are checked against each other before the plan reaches \u201cgood?\u201d\n\nConcrete near-miss while testing: a sample plan headlined cost as **$50\u2013$75**; per-item \u00d7 item count in the same plan was about **$700** (~10\u00d7). The honesty rule bounced the plan for rewrite before approval. Same shape of risk as a real job Jordan cites: cleaning **1.34M** public franchise location records down to ~**60,000** real multi-unit operators, where the final step is a small paid look-up on every row \u2014 a cheap headline over a big row-count job.\n\nHe made this the default for everything he does and shipped it; annual subscribers install the plan style with one command. Geeky rebuild recipe is copyable into Claude Code. Related instinct pointed at: Asymmetry Engine (manufacture more information about a situation than the other side has, then hand it over plainly).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-005"]}, {"n": "13", "subject": "I Scored 16,000 People to Find Who Really Moves a Market. Only 441 Did.", "date": "2026-06-24", "file": "13-i-scored-16-000-people-to-find-who-really-moves.md", "mechanism_short": "A top-down **market influence-graph pipeline** that finds who actually shapes what an industry buys (KOLs / key opinion leaders), then ranks them for advisory-board recruitment \u2014 not from the founder\u2019s CRM contacts, but from the whole market. **Run (regulated public-safety software market):** Named universe ~**16,000** people \u2192 scored on **eight influence dimensions** \u2192 **441** cleared a meaningfu", "mechanism_md": "A top-down **market influence-graph pipeline** that finds who actually shapes what an industry buys (KOLs / key opinion leaders), then ranks them for advisory-board recruitment \u2014 not from the founder\u2019s CRM contacts, but from the whole market.\n\n**Run (regulated public-safety software market):** Named universe ~**16,000** people \u2192 scored on **eight influence dimensions** \u2192 **441** cleared a meaningful influence bar (**<3%**).\n\n**Eight influence dimensions (evidence, not title):** association leadership; government and standards advisory; vendor roles; speaking; authorship; credentials; scale of their platform; reach among peers. Title/org size on the business card counts for very little vs. seat, byline, board, dated documented role (e.g. a retired chief who chairs a national committee can outrank a silent chief of a giant department).\n\n**Discovery (top-down, geographic):** For a US run, 50 states + DC. Region-by-region research agents, each with real tools (web-research engine built for AI, cited-answer research service, public nonprofit filings), answer: who are the most influential people here? Merge sourced shortlists into a draft industry influence graph, then score.\n\n**Capture problem:** Raw influence alone is wrong for board picks. In the run, the single highest-influence person was already on a competing vendor\u2019s payroll (past national-association president, textbook author). Same pattern: standards-committee spokesperson for a competitor; state-association officer in competitor-sponsored sessions. Those names are intel, not candidates.\n\n**Score = Influence \u00d7 Independence \u00d7 Recruitability (multiply, don\u2019t average):**\n- **Influence** \u2014 evidence-based market-shaping score.\n- **Independence** \u2014 vendor/software ties checked against the client\u2019s competitor list; employed by / advising / speaking for a rival \u2192 near-zero independence.\n- **Recruitability** \u2014 realistic win (e.g. retired past-president with time and no exclusive ties vs. sitting exec locked to a competitor).\n\nA near-zero on any axis tanks the product. Outputs **two lists:** (1) **board shortlist** \u2014 independent, recruitable, influential people with evidence why they matter and an approach angle; (2) **competitive-intel list** \u2014 high-influence people competitors already locked up.\n\nFree tier = method only. Paid ($50/mo) = exact pipeline, per-stage tools, rubric, cost discipline (not in this email body).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-017", "U-018"]}, {"n": "14", "subject": "I Used 300 Sales Calls and Search Volume to Pick the Next Webinar", "date": "2026-06-22", "file": "14-i-used-300-sales-calls-and-search-volume-to-pick.md", "mechanism_short": "**Honest webinar attribution + \u201cwhat to run next\u201d demand engine**, built in Claude Code in a day for a demand-gen team at a healthcare compliance / credentialing / training software company (name redacted in the post). They run dozens of webinars a year and needed which topics to keep, kill, and run next. **Problem:** Naive \u201cwebinar-influenced\u201d pipeline (every opp any attendee is on) was fantasy \u2014", "mechanism_md": "**Honest webinar attribution + \u201cwhat to run next\u201d demand engine**, built in Claude Code in a day for a demand-gen team at a healthcare compliance / credentialing / training software company (name redacted in the post). They run dozens of webinars a year and needed which topics to keep, kill, and run next.\n\n**Problem:** Naive \u201cwebinar-influenced\u201d pipeline (every opp any attendee is on) was fantasy \u2014 tens of millions, larger than annual bookings. Failure modes called out:\n- Deals often already existed; >1/5 of \u201cattributed\u201d deals were created *before* the attendee showed up (can\u2019t influence a deal the buyer hadn\u2019t attended yet).\n- Median gap on the rest ~3 months.\n- One deal credited to every webinar anyone on it touched \u2192 double-count across ~100 webinars.\n\n**Dead Salesforce rollups:** Campaign opportunity / won / amount rollups were zero on every webinar campaign (org-wide only a handful of campaigns had anything; won-dollar rollup zero everywhere). Signal: truth lived elsewhere.\n\n**System of record:** Salesforce **Customizable Campaign Influence** \u2014 tens of thousands of influence rows, thousands tied to webinars. Two native credit models:\n- **Sourced** \u2014 single-touch; webinar was primary campaign on the deal; full credit; board-defensible \u201cthis webinar drove this deal.\u201d\n- **Influenced** \u2014 multi-touch; webinar one of several; shared credit; topic reach without inventing attribution.\n\nInflated contact-role / attendee-touch number kept but **relabeled as engagement reach, not pipeline** \u2014 never the headline.\n\n**Four-tab report** (one dataset, sortable/filterable; built to be wrong in places so the team could correct):\n1. **Webinars** \u2014 per webinar: registrants, live vs recording, attendance rate, avg minutes watched, email open/click, sourced + influenced pipeline.\n2. **Topics & products** \u2014 same metrics rolled by product line and webinar type (surfaces \u201cfewer registrants, more conversions\u201d).\n3. **What to run next** \u2014 demand engine (below).\n4. **Deals** \u2014 last-year opps with real revenue + \u22651 webinar attendee at the account; expandable to people/webinars; reach shown beside sourced so the two aren\u2019t confused.\n\n**Demand engine (two passes):** (1) mine real sales/discovery calls for problems in buyers\u2019 words \u2192 rank topic frequency; (2) check those topics against search volume. High on both = layup; high in calls / low search = \u201cown it\u201d (pain without Google vocabulary).\n\n**Meta proof on Jordan\u2019s own data:** 300 of his GTM sales calls + podcast episodes; swarm of **10 agents** clustered themes; each theme checked against live search volume. Mismatch is the product. Example ranked themes (calls intensity \u00b7 monthly searches \u00b7 verdict): reverse-engineer ICP / go narrow (\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 1,600 \u00b7 Run now); outbound messages they\u2019d pay to receive (\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 1,600 \u00b7 Run now); build TAM from public data (\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 10 \u00b7 Own it); AI agents that research accounts at scale (\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 1,600 \u00b7 Run now); score & prioritize TAM (\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 210 \u00b7 Own it); prove outbound worked / attribution (\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 880 \u00b7 Market-led); AI SDRs (\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 1,900 \u00b7 Market-led); GTM engineering without engineers (\u25cf\u25cf \u00b7 2,400 \u00b7 Market-led). Engine put \u201cprove outbound worked \u2014 attribution to pipeline\u201d on the list \u2192 this post.\n\n**Parallel (explicitly out of scope here):** same client\u2019s webinar-audience recovery / re-qualify contacts \u2014 contact discovery machine, not attribution; deferred to its own write-up.\n\nWritten by Claude Opus 4.8, approved by Jordan. Geeky rebuild prompts referenced as below-the-fold (not in this email body).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-015", "U-021"]}, {"n": "15", "subject": "15-why-ai-design-looks-so-generic", "date": "2026-06-18", "file": "15-why-ai-design-looks-so-generic.md", "mechanism_short": "**Steal This Design** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that stops AI UI/copy slop by forcing ground-truth capture instead of model averages. **Problem mechanism:** Asking a model to \"copy Linear's pricing page\" triggers a fetch of page *text* (words, prices, bullets) \u2014 not pixels or CSS. The model never sees the design; it fills gaps from the average of every pricing page it absorbed. That average is the gen", "mechanism_md": "**Steal This Design** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that stops AI UI/copy slop by forcing ground-truth capture instead of model averages.\n\n**Problem mechanism:** Asking a model to \"copy Linear's pricing page\" triggers a fetch of page *text* (words, prices, bullets) \u2014 not pixels or CSS. The model never sees the design; it fills gaps from the average of every pricing page it absorbed. That average is the generic: rounded cards, purple-ish gradient, Inter everywhere.\n\n**Rule: capture, don't describe.** For every reference page worth stealing from:\n1. Render in a real headless browser.\n2. Grab a screenshot (layout + feel).\n3. Extract actual computed CSS (fonts, hex colors, corner radii, card shadows).\n4. Build from those exact values \u2014 not from memory of \"what a pricing page looks like.\"\n\nSkill flow (as described): point at the kind of page you're making \u2192 find best real examples \u2192 headless-render each \u2192 pull a build-ready sheet of exact design values. Demo corpus: fifteen live pricing pages (Linear, Stripe, Vercel, Attio, Figma, Notion, Clerk, Resend, Supabase, Render, Sentry, Webflow, Cal.com, PlanetScale, Liveblocks) \u2014 each captured the same way.\n\n**First-run failure \u2192 rebuild:**\n- v1 shipped a beautiful *design study* (five pricing patterns explained) that buried the decision; wrong shape for the job (\"which one should I ship?\").\n- Real bug: \"Most Popular\" pill absolutely positioned past card edge + card `overflow: clip` \u2192 badge sliced in half.\n- Silent degradation: CSS-extraction step skipped; capture files held only URL + title. Study built from screenshots + model priors \u2014 the slop the skill was meant to kill.\n- Hostile second agent found six problems; Jordan reproduced every claim against live page + real capture files before applying any fix. Habit: finder agent \u2260 confirmer agent; never fix an unreproduced claim.\n\n**Rebuild defaults:**\n- Deliverable = **decision page**, not gallery: recommendation at top (\"I'd ship this one\" + 2\u20133 reasons) \u2192 options with honest verdicts/tradeoffs \u2192 steelmanned runners-up \u2192 fifteen captures demoted to appendix as evidence. Live example: https://playbooks.blueprintgtm.com/steal-this-design\n- **Automated layout gate (bouncer):** headless load at desktop/tablet/phone; fail loud on horizontal scrollbars, broken/zero-size images, recommendation buried too far down, endless phone scroll, and the exact badge-clip pattern. Page does not ship until green.\n- Screenshots inlined into the file (nothing external left to break).\n- CSS extraction **mandatory**; if real values can't be gotten, that reference is dropped (no silent screenshot-only collage).\n\n**Example discovery without listicles:** Exa find-similar seeded with known-good first-party pages (Linear, Stripe, Vercel, Resend, Attio, Framer) returns real pricing pages ranked by how many seeds point at each. Also returns SEO aggregators; skill filters obvious junk, human curates rest \u2192 discover-and-curate, not a verdict.\n\n**Chat-widget gotcha:** some sites (e.g. Airtable) re-assert chat-bubble styles after CSS hide. Fix: delete the chat node entirely in the same instant before screenshot \u2014 not style it away.\n\n**Thesis restated:** don't let the model fill from the average; feed ground truth it couldn't guess. Force the agent to commit (recommendation + machine gate) instead of dodging with option piles.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-023", "U-027"]}, {"n": "16", "subject": "16-sort-all-of-your-customers-by-their-influence", "date": "2026-06-10", "file": "16-sort-all-of-your-customers-by-their-influence.md", "mechanism_short": "Skill **referral-notability**: point it at a customer list; for every account, one Claude subagent (1) finds the owner-operator (never trusts the CRM for the name), (2) reads their full LinkedIn, (3) researches real-world footprint \u2014 association seats, speaking, podcasts, press, awards, review scale \u2014 and (4) applies a fixed rubric to emit a single **0\u2013100 Referral Notability Score**. The score ra", "mechanism_md": "Skill **referral-notability**: point it at a customer list; for every account, one Claude subagent (1) finds the owner-operator (never trusts the CRM for the name), (2) reads their full LinkedIn, (3) researches real-world footprint \u2014 association seats, speaking, podcasts, press, awards, review scale \u2014 and (4) applies a fixed rubric to emit a single **0\u2013100 Referral Notability Score**.\n\nThe score ranks the **owner\u2019s connectedness**, not company size / revenue / seat count. A large quiet institution ranks below a small shop whose owner chairs the state trade association \u2014 because an affiliate/referral program buys vouchability, not logo-wall prestige.\n\nProven on a vertical-SaaS trades customer base (~1,200 accounts, $1,000 account-credit referral program):\n- 1,182 accounts scored \u2192 1,145 unique companies after dedup\n- 56 cleared 55 (affiliate-grade / personal-ask tier); 9 cleared 80\n- Named owner resolved for 1,112 / 1,145 (~97%)\n- Top owners: industry podcast host + national trade-association board + multi-year city award; state association founder/president + national conference speaker + local-TV explainer; mid-list Inc. 5000 + state board + operator-of-the-year \u2014 none were the largest companies in the file\n\nCheap-first research cascade (full recipe paywalled / not in this email body): LinkedIn data lookup already on plan \u2192 ~$0.002 web search \u2192 pricier deep search only when a real thread exists; stop on dry wells (tiny shop, no press, thin profile).\n\nInfrastructure bug caught post-run: evidence files named by CRM ID; 29 Salesforce ID pairs collided on case alone (e.g. `sPSNT` vs `sPSnT`); macOS treated them as one filename \u2192 silent overwrite, no error. Fix: re-key so case can\u2019t collide, re-score affected accounts.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-003", "U-017", "U-018"]}, {"n": "17", "subject": "I Scored 15,489 Account Executives to Find 135 \u2014 for $10. Here's How.", "date": "2026-06-08", "file": "17-i-scored-15-489-account-executives-to-find-135-for.md", "mechanism_short": "**Find a Rep** \u2014 a scorer that ranks AEs by degree-of-fit to a specific sales motion, not by r\u00e9sum\u00e9 prestige (title / years / logo / competitor flag / A-B-C beauty rating). Framework from Matt Braley: same input (r\u00e9sum\u00e9) gets opposite verdicts depending on role context. A prior sourcer fixed \u201cwho do we look at\u201d (search every company, don\u2019t sample fifty); this rebuild fixes \u201chow do we rank them.\u201d E", "mechanism_md": "**Find a Rep** \u2014 a scorer that ranks AEs by degree-of-fit to a specific sales motion, not by r\u00e9sum\u00e9 prestige (title / years / logo / competitor flag / A-B-C beauty rating). Framework from Matt Braley: same input (r\u00e9sum\u00e9) gets opposite verdicts depending on role context. A prior sourcer fixed \u201cwho do we look at\u201d (search every company, don\u2019t sample fifty); this rebuild fixes \u201chow do we rank them.\u201d\n\nEight dimensions, each scored **aligned / partial / not** against the target role (not pass/fail gates):\n1. Company stage sold from (seed scrappiness vs late-stage brand door-opening)\n2. Type of buyer (CRO signer vs self-serve user vs procurement committee)\n3. Sales motion (new-logo outbound vs product-led self-serve vs expansion book)\n4. Sales cycle length (two weeks vs nine months)\n5. Number of buyers in a deal (one DM vs five-person committee / multi-threading)\n6. Cross-departmental complexity (RevOps, Finance, IT, Security pulled in)\n7. Deal size ($8K transactional vs six-/seven-figure ACV)\n8. Industry (horizontal vs regulated vertical / jargon depth)\n\nA rep can be 8-of-8 for one job and 2-of-8 for the same title elsewhere.\n\n**Client run (\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588 \u2014 GTM software, Series A\u2013C / 30\u2013200 person world; needed new-logo AE closing complex six-figure committee deals into revenue leaders):**\n- Blitz API: every AE at 30\u2013200-person U.S. software cos \u2192 **15,489** people\n- Deduped employers \u2192 **6,960** companies; website read kept only GTM/sales-tech sellers to revenue leaders \u2192 **671** cos (670 model + 1 hand-added) \u2192 **1,563** AEs\n- Full LinkedIn history on **1,428**; deterministic tenure rules (\u22654 years closing as AE with SDR time excluded; 3+ years in seat; 4\u20138 years total; no job-hopping) \u2192 **135** survivors\n- All 135 scored on the eight dimensions and ranked\n- **11** clean 8-of-8; **35** kept as \u201cflex\u201d near-misses (one soft miss shouldn\u2019t bury an otherwise-perfect rep); screen questions flagged per open dimension\n- Top examples cited by motion fit (not logo): Alex Holler (Tackle.io), August Buettner (HG Insights), Christopher Trebon (Canidium) \u2014 all 8-of-8 cousin sellers to revenue leaders", "upgrade_ids": ["U-004", "U-009", "U-025", "U-026"]}, {"n": "18", "subject": "A TAM of named contacts for $0", "date": "2026-06-04", "file": "18-a-tam-of-named-contacts-for-0.md", "mechanism_short": "**Lynx** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (`/edge install tam-contact-harvester`) that turns a CSV of company domains into a labeled contact list (email + person) by reading free HTML first and only paying for the residue. **Classify-before-you-pay triage.** Before extraction, hit each homepage and bucket by response body (not status code \u2014 Cloudflare challenges and empty React shells both return 200): 1. Em", "mechanism_md": "**Lynx** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (`/edge install tam-contact-harvester`) that turns a CSV of company domains into a labeled contact list (email + person) by reading free HTML first and only paying for the residue.\n\n**Classify-before-you-pay triage.** Before extraction, hit each homepage and bucket by response body (not status code \u2014 Cloudflare challenges and empty React shells both return 200):\n1. Email already in raw HTML (best)\n2. Plain server-rendered text (easy)\n3. Empty JavaScript shell (needs render)\n4. Cloudflare / bot-wall (hard)\n5. Dead domain\n\n**Deterministic free extraction** (majority never touches an LLM): `mailto:` links; Cloudflare\u2019s hex-encoded email (one-byte XOR, reversible without running their JS); Schema.org / JSON-LD (name, title, email); team-card \u201cMeet the Team\u201d structure (photo\u2013name\u2013title\u2013email). Only genuinely ambiguous pages (e.g. six people, four emails, no clean tie) go to a model.\n\n**Named person vs generic mailbox.** Every email is labeled person-tied or generic (`info@`); only person-tied count. Free-route named-contact floor ~24% on both markets. Cheap second pass: re-read already-downloaded pages with **Gemini Flash-Lite** for emails still missing a name (no re-crawl). Acupuncture: named rate 37% \u2192 50% of contact list (+60 people). Lawyers: +6 (team pages already had attorney names next to emails).\n\n**Two-stage name\u2194email gates** (anti-fabrication): (1) deterministic \u2014 first and last name words must literally appear in page text (catches reverse-engineered names from `first.last@`); (2) adversarial second model \u2014 prove the pairing is wrong; default \u201cwrong\u201d if unsure. After gates: **100%** verified accuracy (50/50 acupuncture, 6/6 lawyers); before gates 98% \u2014 the 2% caught were fabrications.\n\n**Paid residue tier** (separate, hard dollar cap): Cloudflare-walled sites and pages with no named owner \u2014 contacts API, render service, or Common Crawl archive. Live breakages called out: billing bug counting failed API calls as spend; contacts API returning 401 on the one needed endpoint while others worked.\n\n**Demo runs (both $0.00 free pass):** acupuncture 500 domains \u2192 68% page-resolution, 456 contacts (31% email-in-HTML + 38% static \u2248 69% free; 20% Cloudflare; 6% JS). Lawyers 150 domains \u2192 63% page-resolution. Method travels within ~5 points.\n\n**How to run (from post):** `python3 scripts/harvest.py --input your-market.csv --out run/` \u2192 `enrich_names.py` \u2192 `verify_names.py` \u2192 optional `tier1.py --run \u2026 --budget 5 --source apify`.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-003", "U-004", "U-005", "U-010", "U-011", "U-024"]}, {"n": "19", "subject": "I scored every parking lot in America for $45", "date": "2026-05-30", "file": "19-i-scored-every-parking-lot-in-america-for-45.md", "mechanism_short": "**Asset-first parking-lot lead pipeline** \u2014 invert cold outreach: don\u2019t start from federal industry codes (what a business is); start from the lot (shape + condition visible from above), then only for the worst lots find the owner who can sign for repair. Concrete Dallas run (free preview): 1. Pull commercial parking-lot polygons from **OpenStreetMap** (already drawn). 2. Filter with free **public", "mechanism_md": "**Asset-first parking-lot lead pipeline** \u2014 invert cold outreach: don\u2019t start from federal industry codes (what a business is); start from the lot (shape + condition visible from above), then only for the worst lots find the owner who can sign for repair.\n\nConcrete Dallas run (free preview):\n1. Pull commercial parking-lot polygons from **OpenStreetMap** (already drawn).\n2. Filter with free **public building footprints** to drop rooftops before any AI spend.\n3. Crop a **free aerial photo** of each remaining polygon.\n4. Ask a **cheap AI** for pavement condition **0\u2013100**; gate out \u201cnot actually a lot.\u201d\n5. Rank; treat only the worst as leads; find owner **after** scoring.\n\nDallas numbers in the post: OSM had **1,355** commercial lots over **50,000 sq ft**; building-footprint filter threw out **236** rooftops; cheap model gated **332** as not-a-lot; **787** got a real condition score; Dallas AI bill **~\\$1**. Spread: **52 Poor**, **656 Fair**, **61 Satisfactory**, **18 Good** (freshly sealed). **52** scored **\u226455** = the leads; flagging everything under **70** yields **720** (every aging lot = noise). Lot sizes in the same run: **3.8-acre** corner strip to **27-acre** vehicle yard \u2014 same pipeline, no per-lot tuning.\n\nHonesty rule: free aerial **cannot** resolve a single pothole (smaller than one pixel). What free photos *can* show is neglect signatures \u2014 faded paint lines, sealcoat fading black\u2192grey\u2192brown, big repair patches, dark water-pool stains, alligator-crack webs. Post cites a **2024 study** at **93%** accuracy on a five-bucket condition rating from photos this coarse (study admitted it couldn\u2019t see individual cracks). Match the claim to the pixels; buy sharper imagery only for the handful you\u2019re about to bid.\n\nCorroboration (no ask required): overlay Dallas **311 street-repair complaints** on scores \u2014 lots called bad averaged **8.3** nearby complaints; good ones **5.2**.\n\nFree imagery ceiling surprise: **NAIP** (federal, whole-country, **60 cm**) is not the free ceiling. About **35 states** publish their own aerials sharper \u2014 most **15 cm**, several (**Connecticut, Kentucky, Rhode Island, DC**) at **7.5 cm** \u2014 on state GIS servers. Post says the paid tier covers cheapest scoring cascade, exact endpoints, and the legal move that makes it shippable; those details are **not** in this free email.\n\nWritten by Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context), approved by Jordan. Subject frames America-scale cost as **\\$45**; free body only itemizes the Dallas **~\\$1** path.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-024"]}, {"n": "20", "subject": "20-automated-3-deep-research-agents-claude-chatgpt-gemini-0-api", "date": "2026-05-27", "file": "20-automated-3-deep-research-agents-claude-chatgpt-gemini-0-api.md", "mechanism_short": "**Triple Deep Research** \u2014 one research question fan-out across ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini deep-research at the same time, then a Claude agent wave that fact-checks every cited link and synthesizes one report where disagreements that survive verification lead as the asymmetric edge. Concrete flow: 1. User feeds one open-web question (e.g. state-level Medicare Advantage reimbursement rules in 2025", "mechanism_md": "**Triple Deep Research** \u2014 one research question fan-out across ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini deep-research at the same time, then a Claude agent wave that fact-checks every cited link and synthesizes one report where disagreements that survive verification lead as the asymmetric edge.\n\nConcrete flow:\n1. User feeds one open-web question (e.g. state-level Medicare Advantage reimbursement rules in 2025; B2B SaaS that shipped AI agents then publicly walked them back).\n2. Tool fans the same question to all three engines in parallel (ChatGPT/Bing index, Claude\u2019s own index, Gemini/Google corpus).\n3. Each engine returns a deep-research report from its own web slice.\n4. Claude agent wave atomizes claims, verifies cites against live URLs, date-checks, ranks usefulness, and writes a final report with stable claim IDs (e.g. `[claude-042]`, `[chatgpt-014]`) resolvable to raw page text on disk, plus a verification appendix (dead links, hallucinated cites, date flags, contradictions).\n5. Claims that survived verification *and* came from only one engine lead the report (set-difference = asymmetric edge). Consensus-all-three claims are baseline, not insight.\n\n**Browser write / JSON read path (why scrapers fail):** Platforms block naive automation (ChatGPT signal-validation + math puzzle + Cloudflare; Claude connector + scope modals; Gemini chunked envelope that expires). Approach: drive the user\u2019s real signed-in Chrome for writes; from inside that tab, call each platform\u2019s undocumented data feed the page itself uses for reads (plain text / full citations). Proof point: Claude path returns full ~31,213-char report + 60 citations in 157ms vs DOM scraper getting 1/427 links. Extraction is decoupled: browser saves raw page + response to disk first; extractor runs offline so UI breakage does not force a re-run (quota + 15\u201355 min wall time).\n\n**Fail-fast partial Triple:** One platform timeout/quota/modal failure exits with clear status; fact-checker wave runs on the 1\u20133 platforms that succeeded. Degraded but still useful.\n\n**When not to invoke (from post):** quick factual lookups; code/repo search; account-specific CRM/dossier work (use Dossier Builder skill instead); anything needing answer in under five minutes.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-019", "U-020"]}, {"n": "21", "subject": "A Tool That Audits All of Your Customer Data", "date": "2026-05-26", "file": "21-a-tool-that-audits-all-of-your-customer-data.md", "mechanism_short": "**Dossier Data Auditor** \u2014 a pre-flight tool that checks customer data *before* Dossier Builder runs. If data is broken, it stops the build; if fine, it hands the next tool a clean list of what to use and what to skip. Auditor and builder talk through a **single handshake file** (auditor writes, builder reads; no hand-copy). A **customer dossier** (from his existing Dossier Builder) is a one-page ", "mechanism_md": "**Dossier Data Auditor** \u2014 a pre-flight tool that checks customer data *before* Dossier Builder runs. If data is broken, it stops the build; if fine, it hands the next tool a clean list of what to use and what to skip. Auditor and builder talk through a **single handshake file** (auditor writes, builder reads; no hand-copy).\n\nA **customer dossier** (from his existing Dossier Builder) is a one-page per-account report that lines up CRM, billing, call recordings, product analytics, and support tickets on a timeline and states in plain English whether the account is active, about to churn, and who they actually talk to. Builder only works if input data is good \u2014 and it almost never is.\n\nThree failure modes the auditor targets:\n1. **Bad joins / ID matches** across systems (CRM \u2194 billing \u2194 call recorder). Example cited: **282** customer records matched to the wrong account via a sloppy \u201csame email\u201d rule; every downstream report wrong.\n2. **Too many same-meaning columns** (e.g. six Salesforce \u201crevenue\u201d fields \u2014 Annual Revenue, MRR, Monthly Revenue Summary, Reported Revenue, Subscription Revenue, Annual Revenue Range \u2014 disagreeing, stale ~18 months, none labeled canonical). Wrong pick \u2192 wrong dossier \u2192 CEO closes report.\n3. **Duplicate / split company identity** \u2014 legal name, brand, DBA as separate accounts (activity split); worse: company listed as its own parent.\n\nNormal workflow (build \u2192 find bugs \u2192 fix \u2192 rebuild) wastes a day. Auditor reverses order: find all three problems first; write exactly what\u2019s broken; **refuse to let Dossier Builder run** until data is fixed or operator overrides with \u201cI know, do it anyway.\u201d\n\n**Load-bearing rule: math first, AI second \u2014 never reverse.** Small Python script counts matches (e.g. column A \u2194 B), writes the real number to disk (hand-checkable). AI only reads that number and labels **pass / warning / soft fail / hard fail** \u2014 opinion allowed, inventing the number forbidden.\n\n**Strict check order** (cannot flip):\n1. **Domains** \u2014 ground-truth brand vs corporate vs multi-domain ownership.\n2. **Joins** \u2014 test matches between systems; discard those that fail a real test.\n3. **Revenue** \u2014 pick canonical revenue column; skip do-not-use; ensure picked column has data.\n4. **Sanity** \u2014 impossible combos (positive revenue + zero calls in six months; columns untouched for years; self-referencing parents).\n\nFake-data test catches: join column only **40%** valid (**60%** wrong) \u2192 hard failure; one company as its own parent \u2192 flagged for repair; do-not-use \u201cMonthly Revenue Summary\u201d skipped in favor of **MRR**.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-022"]}, {"n": "22", "subject": "A Claude Code skill for AI audits", "date": "2026-05-18", "file": "22-a-claude-code-skill-for-ai-audits.md", "mechanism_short": "**AI Deployment Report skill** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that turns three inputs into one CEO-grade HTML deck (~15-minute read, ~30 minutes to generate) answering: Is the Claude investment paying off? Who ships vs who chats? Who should be on the leaderboard next quarter? **Three inputs \u2192 one HTML page** 1. **Anthropic chat-history export ZIP** \u2014 individuals via claude.ai \u2192 Settings \u2192 Data privacy cont", "mechanism_md": "**AI Deployment Report skill** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that turns three inputs into one CEO-grade HTML deck (~15-minute read, ~30 minutes to generate) answering: Is the Claude investment paying off? Who ships vs who chats? Who should be on the leaderboard next quarter?\n\n**Three inputs \u2192 one HTML page**\n1. **Anthropic chat-history export ZIP** \u2014 individuals via claude.ai \u2192 Settings \u2192 Data privacy controls \u2192 Export; org admins via console.anthropic.com \u2192 Privacy \u2192 Export organization data (ZIP arrives by email; drop in `~/Downloads/`).\n2. **Local clones of every git repo** where the team ships Claude-assisted code \u2014 skill walks every commit across every repo, attributes by email, separates partner vs staff commits, narrates multi-repo cutovers in one line, surfaces real code writers vs chat experimenters. Northwind demo: two repos, four months of history.\n3. **Anthropic admin API key** (`sk-ant-admin-\u2026` from console.anthropic.com \u2192 Settings \u2192 Admin API keys). Rotate when the run finishes. Without it: people-and-shipping story only. With it: also dollars-and-infrastructure (total spend, per-key concentration, dormant credentials, daily cost curve, prompt caching on/off, Claude Code OpenTelemetry exporter wired or not).\n\n**Run shape (as stated):**\n```\nexport ANTHROPIC_ADMIN_KEY=sk-ant-admin-...\nbash run.sh ~/Downloads/data-<export-id>-batch-0000.zip --client <your-client> --publish\n```\nDeck lands at `playbooks.blueprintgtm.com/<your-slug>`, JS password-gated until the team gets the code.\n\n**Eleven sections (grouped as in post)**\n- **Where are we (1\u20132):** Anchor KPIs + headline gap (Northwind: 91 regular Claude users, 3 ship code); daily conversation curve + top five contributors by volume.\n- **Money (3):** Daily spend curve, monthly totals, per-key concentration, dormant credentials, prompt-caching status, Claude Code OTel exporter status. Northwind: four-month bill **$2,564** across 9 API keys / 1 workspace; **$671** spike on March 26 from a 5,090-account churn analysis. Section 3 only renders when admin key was set. Cost-report API `amount` is **cents not USD** \u2014 first pass nearly shipped 100\u00d7-wrong totals; fix lives at `scripts/cost_units.py` via `cents_to_usd()` (companion piece: the $67K bill that wasn\u2019t).\n- **Who\u2019s shipping (4\u20135):** Shipping-gap table (chat-active users vs commits/lines/chat overlap; partners vs staff); leaderboard with archetype pill, six-attribute radar (1\u20135 from conversation evidence + commit subjects), verbatim quote, two-sentence exec read. Archetypes (descriptive): Builder, Frontier Explorer, Workflow Embedder, Methodical Operator, Coach, Power User. Attributes: deep functional expertise, creative problem solver, organized and structured, driver, trusted peer, learns fast.\n- **Functional AI Lead candidates (6):** Featured \u201clead in action\u201d cards for departments that already have a federated AI lead; ranked candidate pool (rationale, risk, next step) for those that don\u2019t \u2014 CEO closes with people to promote.\n- **What\u2019s repeatable (7\u201310):** Top projects (knowledge files so new hires can pick up); theme map + top contributors per theme; recurring use cases (Tiger Team productization targets); best-prompts gallery (12 exemplary prompts + one-line \u201cwhy this is good\u201d).\n- **Action (11):** 9 concrete 60-day moves (6 people/process + 3 Anthropic-spend moves auto-injected when admin API was set); each one sentence.\n\n**`wtf` polish stage:** Every LLM prose block runs through pipeline stage `10_wtf_polish.py` before render \u2014 kills AI tells (delve, robust, leverage-as-verb, em-dash+negation, \u201cX. Not Y.\u201d, BLUF-at-the-end); forces engineering nouns \u2192 business outcomes. Verbatim quotes, sample prompts, evidence citations, numbers, names: never touched. ~$2 and ~30 seconds per deck; no skip version.\n\nPublic demo: anonymized Northwind Health AI Deployment Report at playbooks.blueprintgtm.com/ai-deployment-report-public-demo (real client run, names swapped, KPIs ranged).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-005"]}, {"n": "23", "subject": "23-your-llm-writes-3-000-words-your-cro-reads-for", "date": "2026-05-14", "file": "23-your-llm-writes-3-000-words-your-cro-reads-for.md", "mechanism_short": "Jordan shipped an **Editorial Frame** skill that turns bloated LLM exec briefs into 30-second reads. Origin story: Claude produced a 3,214-word churn brief with the recommendation on page 4 and no action layer; he hand-cut it to 1,180 words (recommendation in sentence one, \u201cSo what\u201d block under every section, one chart kept of eight). After repeating the same five edits three times in a week, he p", "mechanism_md": "Jordan shipped an **Editorial Frame** skill that turns bloated LLM exec briefs into 30-second reads. Origin story: Claude produced a 3,214-word churn brief with the recommendation on page 4 and no action layer; he hand-cut it to 1,180 words (recommendation in sentence one, \u201cSo what\u201d block under every section, one chart kept of eight). After repeating the same five edits three times in a week, he productized them.\n\n**Mechanism:**\n1. **Editorial Frame (five questions, every section scored 1\u20135):** (1) Find what\u2019s important, (2) Cut what doesn\u2019t need to be there, (3) Surface what the reader will actually do, (4) Specify how they take action, (5) Reorder so the answer is first. Default verdict is `cut`; a section only `keep`s when it earns it.\n2. **Canon mapped to those questions:** Tufte data-ink \u2192 Q1; Doumont signal-to-noise \u2192 Q2; military BLUF \u2192 Q3\u2013Q4; Minto Pyramid \u2192 Q5. No extra frameworks or PowerPoint templates \u2014 answer: what do I do, why, and by when?\n3. **Lint engine on skill output (LLM-tell catcher, not code lint):** Twelve hard-hit patterns block publish \u2014 reversal cliffhanger; antithesis tic; throat-clearing opener; recap signature; filler transitions; rigid optimism close; imagine-a-world opener; magic-adverb verbs; vague attribution; schema leaks; inline code chips; em-dash overuse (>0.15 per sentence; cites GPT-4.1 at 3.3\u00d7 human rate). Plus banned AI vocab list (delve, leverage, robust, seamless, holistic, synergy, paradigm, empower, harness, streamline, cutting-edge, best-in-class, ecosystem, game-changer, unlock, elevate, disrupt, tapestry, landscape, notably, moreover, furthermore, utilize, underscore, pivotal, transcend, navigate, testament, realm). Hard hit \u2192 one auto-retry with lint piped into rewriter; fail twice \u2192 halt and surface draft + hit list; human edits or overrides with written reason \u2014 never silent bypass.\n4. **Chart sub-agent (Tufte-first, twelve rules, first match wins):** single number \u2192 no chart; two-number ratio \u2192 bold in prose; prose already carries comparison \u2192 no chart; time series \u22645 points \u2192 sparkline; categorical \u22647 ranked \u2192 horizontal bar desc; categorical 8\u201325 \u2192 Cleveland dot plot; pie \u2192 always bar; chart only when removing it loses info prose can\u2019t carry. Constraints from Cleveland, Few, Bertin, Knaflic, Cairo. Required charts \u2192 auto-upgrade output to HTML (warning printed; styled like playbooks.blueprintgtm.com).\n5. **Hard non-goals:** no AI hero images; hard cap 60% of original word count (output >65% re-fired \u201ccut harder\u201d); methodology sections flagged `cut` first; skill never mentions itself in the polished brief.\n6. **Customer voice:** internal version pulls last meeting transcript from Jordan\u2019s Sybill stash for the company; voice rewriter weaves customer concerns/vocab. Shipped subscriber version: `--transcript path/to/meeting-export.md` (Sybill, Gong, Otter, Fathom, raw notes, any text). Output: local file + polished markdown auto-copied to clipboard for paste into Google Doc / Substack / email / Slack.\n\nThesis stated: most \u201cAI bloat\u201d is an editorial problem industrialized by cheap generation; the skill makes editorial discipline mechanical for the CRO\u2019s 30-second window.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-021", "U-023", "U-028"]}, {"n": "24", "subject": "Stop sampling candidates. Search them all.", "date": "2026-05-12", "file": "24-stop-sampling-candidates-search-them-all.md", "mechanism_short": "**talent-sourcer** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (annual install: `/edge install talent-sourcer`) that rebuilds a Sunday-afternoon recruiter hack into a reusable pipeline. Trigger: Series B healthcare AI company needed an SDR leader; they wanted real candidates, not a recruiting-agency $50K / 90-day resume stack. **v1 hack flow:** Point Blitz API at every company in the filter \u2192 LinkedIn employee-finder p", "mechanism_md": "**talent-sourcer** \u2014 a Claude Code skill (annual install: `/edge install talent-sourcer`) that rebuilds a Sunday-afternoon recruiter hack into a reusable pipeline. Trigger: Series B healthcare AI company needed an SDR leader; they wanted real candidates, not a recruiting-agency $50K / 90-day resume stack.\n\n**v1 hack flow:** Point Blitz API at every company in the filter \u2192 LinkedIn employee-finder per company \u2192 Claude scores candidates \u2192 RapidAPI scrapes fresh profiles on survivors \u2192 Monday morning ranked Google Sheet (emails, phones, per-person narratives). Then rebuilt as a skill anyone with the two API keys can run.\n\n**Core architecture shift (same as bad outbound):** Default sourcing samples ~50 obvious companies (competitors + category leaders \u2192 Apollo \u2192 scroll). Blitz is flat-rate (**$399/mo** unlimited people + company search); only hard constraint is QPS (5 for most accounts; 20\u219250 agency tier). So: **don\u2019t sample \u2014 search every company that fits the filter.** SDR-leader case: **9,000+ NYC companies** in the headcount band; every plausible person from each. ~**4 hours** compute on slow tier; **~30 minutes** on fast. Framed as the asymmetry engine applied to hiring: manufacture more information on every plausible candidate than the agency has, deliver ranked shortlist as a permissionless gift.\n\n**Competitor bonus:** Scoring agents usually grade the person alone (skills, tenure, trajectory, location). Extra dimension: if the candidate works at a **direct competitor** of the hiring company, automatic ranking boost + explicit flag in output (already knows buyers, objections, customers).\n\n**Score company first, then person:** Before any candidate scoring, enrich the employer \u2014 Blitz company data (headcount, industry, stage) + separate worker downloads the homepage (**30 companies in parallel**). Scoring agent reads both and grades the person in employer context (\u201cVP Sales\u201d at a 50-person healthcare AI co \u2260 \u201cVP Sales\u201d at Oracle).\n\n**Deliverables:** Two \u201canyone with link can view\u201d URLs \u2014 (1) three-tab Google Sheet: ranked candidates, company context, search filters; (2) Google Doc: executive summary + per-candidate narratives for the **top thirty**. Trade vs agency: agency **$20\u201350K** and **three months**; pipeline = two API subscriptions + an afternoon, and it flags which candidates currently sit at direct competitors.\n\nGeeky rebuild recipe sits below the fold (copy into Claude Code); annual path is the shipped skill.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-009", "U-025"]}, {"n": "25", "subject": "If your ICP is just a filter, you have nothing to say", "date": "2026-05-09", "file": "25-if-your-icp-is-just-a-filter-you-have-nothing.md", "mechanism_short": "Skill **`win-loss-rewind`**: ICP inverted. Start from labeled outcomes you already have (won, lost, healthy, churned, expanded), reconstruct what those companies looked like **before** they bought (six to eighteen months prior), hunt public-data signals that separate good-fit from bad-fit, and ship a **per-archetype scoring rubric** instead of a one-line firmographic (\u201c50\u20131,000 employees in vertic", "mechanism_md": "Skill **`win-loss-rewind`**: ICP inverted. Start from labeled outcomes you already have (won, lost, healthy, churned, expanded), reconstruct what those companies looked like **before** they bought (six to eighteen months prior), hunt public-data signals that separate good-fit from bad-fit, and ship a **per-archetype scoring rubric** instead of a one-line firmographic (\u201c50\u20131,000 employees in vertical X\u201d = Apollo filter, not ICP).\n\n**Phase 0 (data confidence \u2014 required):** Cross-check CRM labels against external reality before any hypothesis work \u2014 company exists on LinkedIn, website resolves, headcount matches CRM. Failures are quarantined; operator reviews/approves a `data_confidence.md` before the rest of the pipeline runs. Motivation: CRM \u201c$50K ARR, healthy, expansion in flight\u201d records that Firecrawl + LinkedIn enrichment show as one-employee shells with dead sites poison the discriminator.\n\n**Deliverable shape (fictional GRC SaaS fixture shipped with the skill):** Two hidden archetypes, different signal recipes at the same stage/revenue band.\n\nExample archetype \u2014 *Two-state legal-ops shop, no in-house engineer*:\n- Signal 1: state professional registrations in prior 18mo (state SoS portal), threshold \u22652, weight 0.55, lift 4.1x train / 3.6x holdout\n- Signal 2: compliance/GRC role posted in prior 6mo (Firecrawl careers keyword), threshold present, weight 0.45, lift 3.5x train / 3.2x holdout\n- `fit_score = 0.55 * I(reg) + 0.45 * I(role)`; \u201clooks like archetype\u201d if `fit_score >= 0.60`\n\nSecond fixture archetype: engineering shops whose compliance-tagged GitHub repos went quiet ~nine months after the maintainer left \u2014 same stage/revenue, different recipe. No headcount/vertical/revenue in either rubric.\n\n**Hard gates + holdout:** Hypothesis generator produces many candidates; Phase 5 kills ones that don\u2019t prove pain (and rejects headcount/industry/revenue as discriminators). Phase 6 holds out 20% from the start (never shown to hypothesis-gen) and re-tests survivors \u2014 e.g. \u201ccompany names start with vowels\u201d (~2.4x train lift on small set) collapses to lift \u22481.0 on holdout and dies; real signals keep lift (4.1\u21923.6, 3.5\u21923.2).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-006", "U-008"]}, {"n": "26", "subject": "26-the-pdf-contact-extractor-i-built-discovers-sources-pulls-every", "date": "2026-05-07", "file": "26-the-pdf-contact-extractor-i-built-discovers-sources-pulls-every.md", "mechanism_short": "**PDF Contact Extractor** \u2014 end-to-end tool that discovers contact-rich PDFs (state licensing boards, federal agency rosters, trade association member lists, conference attendee PDFs, etc.), downloads them, triages text vs scanned, extracts every contact row, merges/dedupes, and audits coverage. **Core insight (from `video-list-extractor`, applied to PDFs):** naive whole-table vision OCR on long c", "mechanism_md": "**PDF Contact Extractor** \u2014 end-to-end tool that discovers contact-rich PDFs (state licensing boards, federal agency rosters, trade association member lists, conference attendee PDFs, etc.), downloads them, triages text vs scanned, extracts every contact row, merges/dedupes, and audits coverage.\n\n**Core insight (from `video-list-extractor`, applied to PDFs):** naive whole-table vision OCR on long contact lists fails (~58% accuracy; Gemini invents org names; Claude drops names, swaps emails, over-normalizes titles). Structural fix: crop each row into its own image and OCR one at a time \u2192 **99% accuracy** with the same model/prompt.\n\n**Smart path \u2014 don\u2019t OCR what you don\u2019t have to:** most contact PDFs have a native text layer. `pypdf` reads them at zero LLM cost. Triage scores text quality (example: NJ fire marshal directory + 798-page NJ fire department roster both scored **1.0** \u2192 text path).\n\n**Eight-phase orchestrator pipeline (scripts in sequence):**\n1. **Pre-flight** \u2014 validate Serper + Exa keys; ask industry + desired fields.\n2. **Discovery** \u2014 five parallel sub-agents hit federal databases, state registries, industry associations, Google dorking patterns, and open-data portals; write candidate PDF URLs to JSON.\n3. **Download** \u2014 stream each PDF with a **30 MB** cap to `/tmp/pcf_pdfs/`.\n4. **Triage** \u2014 score text quality; route to text path or vision path.\n5. **Contact-page detection** \u2014 for big PDFs, keep pages that actually have contacts; skip TOC/intro.\n6. **Extract** \u2014 text: `pypdf` + regex; vision: render page at **200 DPI**, crop contact rows by ink-density projection, dedupe crops, dispatch parallel OCR sub-agents (waves of **5**, **14** batches \u2014 same architecture as `video-list-extractor`).\n7. **Merge** \u2014 combine text + vision outputs, dedupe across sources, write clean CSV.\n8. **Audit** \u2014 sample random pages, independent verifier agents, coverage check; target **\u226597%**.\n\n**Demo result:** two PDFs (~820 pages combined), both text-routed \u2192 **5,080** raw records \u2192 **2,015** unique fire-department contacts in under a minute; LLM cost **$0.00** (discovery skipped \u2014 URLs already known). Same contacts via vision path estimated **~$4\u20136**; naive vision-everything path ~**$25**. Triage is the cost lever (90% text-extractable runs cost ~5\u00d7 less than 90% scanned).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-011", "U-012"]}, {"n": "27", "subject": "Stop Buying Lists. Build Them.", "date": "2026-05-06", "file": "27-stop-buying-lists-build-them.md", "mechanism_short": "**TAM research skill** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that, for any narrow company type, discovers where the vertical\u2019s truth actually lives (gov registries, associations, Maps, open data), scores sources on a fixed rubric, and recommends a 2\u20133 source combo instead of buying aggregator lists. Concrete landscaper / tree-care case (one pass): - **38 sources** surfaced. Foursquare alone: **97,043** unique lan", "mechanism_md": "**TAM research skill** \u2014 a Claude Code skill that, for any narrow company type, discovers where the vertical\u2019s truth actually lives (gov registries, associations, Maps, open data), scores sources on a fixed rubric, and recommends a 2\u20133 source combo instead of buying aggregator lists.\n\nConcrete landscaper / tree-care case (one pass):\n- **38 sources** surfaced. Foursquare alone: **97,043** unique landscapers/tree services, phones for **99.8%**, websites for **64%**. California contractor licensing board free CSV: **~12,000** licensed landscapers aggregators missed. Two trade-association member directories: **~5,400** of the companies that actually buy software.\n- Adjacent-license trick: every state requires a **pesticide-applicator license** for commercial weed/pest spray \u2014 a \u201cpesticide\u201d registry is a 50-state public roster of professional landscapers, free, law-maintained. Spot this by asking what licenses the company must hold, not by searching the company type by name.\n- Combined, **three** of those sources \u2192 **~85%** of addressable market for **~$0** data cost. Client\u2019s aggregator ($40K/yr) had captured **~60%** of the same vertical with no way to see the missing 40%.\n\n**Five phases (in order):**\n0. **Kill switch** \u2014 validate every API key the run depends on before work; five keys, one fails \u2192 whole run stops (built after a 3-hour run died at minute 175 when Apify\u2019s token rotated).\n1. **Silent client-site read** \u2014 pull what they sell and to whom; no questions yet so phase two can ask sharp questions.\n2. **Narrow** \u2014 only interactive phase; forces one company type (\u201cAmbulatory surgery centers\u201d / \u201cDirect primary care clinics,\u201d not \u201cHealthcare providers\u201d). Refuses to launch discovery until a subagent could find every instance.\n3. **Five parallel subagents** \u2014 federal registries; state licensing boards; industry associations + niche directories; Google Maps / local business APIs; open data / web datasets. Each returns structured JSON with the same fields (coverage, record count, websites included, cost, quirks).\n4. **Score + recommend** \u2014 same rubric across ~38 sources \u2192 ranked table + recommended two-or-three-source combination (~85% market, minimal overlap). Deduplicate combined sources on **phone number**.\n\nWhole run: **~45 minutes**, **$2\u2013$10** in API calls.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-010", "U-011"]}, {"n": "28", "subject": "28-find-every-customer-of-any-technology", "date": "2026-05-05", "file": "28-find-every-customer-of-any-technology.md", "mechanism_short": "Shipped tool that, given a **product name + its domain**, returns a **master CSV of every confirmed customer** of that technology \u2014 not \"companies that might use it.\" **Problem that drove it:** Client asked for every company using a specific warehouse management system, with proof. Default stack (BuiltWith, G2 reviews, job postings naming the product, LinkedIn posts, analyst reports) only yields *", "mechanism_md": "Shipped tool that, given a **product name + its domain**, returns a **master CSV of every confirmed customer** of that technology \u2014 not \"companies that might use it.\"\n\n**Problem that drove it:** Client asked for every company using a specific warehouse management system, with proof. Default stack (BuiltWith, G2 reviews, job postings naming the product, LinkedIn posts, analyst reports) only yields *probable* users: one-time trials, stale 18-month job ads after a switch, G2 reviewers at consultancies who evaluated for a client. Inferred lists; no way to tell live use.\n\n**Thesis:** Confirmed signals beat inferred signals. A clickable URL beats a BuiltWith tag. A certificate-transparency log entry beats a G2 review. A working password-reset page beats a LinkedIn mention. If a company uses the product *right now*, that fact shows up in **infrastructure** \u2014 DNS records, SSL certificates, HTTP headers, login pages \u2014 not marketing copy or job boards.\n\n**Named pattern: fingerprint first, harvest second.**\n1. **Fingerprint** \u2014 Deeply study how the technology manifests in the wild: HTTP headers it sets, DNS patterns it follows, HTML shape, cookies it drops, login-page / auth-endpoint behavior when probed with an email that doesn't exist (diff valid vs invalid instances, raw HTTP responses). Example given: a product with tenants at `companyname.<product-domain>` \u2014 resolve DNS, hit the URL; a **200 OK is binary proof**, not a \"probably.\"\n2. **Harvest** \u2014 Only after the fingerprint exists, scan for it. Fingerprint research is the boring hours-per-product moat; keyword matching (what everyone else does) skips it and gets guesses.\n\n**Autonomous pipeline the tool runs:** fingerprint the product \u2192 harvest candidates from **six different sources** \u2192 probe them at scale \u2192 extract company identity from each confirmed instance \u2192 enrich with real firmographic data \u2192 emit master CSV of confirmed customers.\n\nAuthor note: written by Claude Opus 4.7, approved by Jordan. Full geeky rebuild copy is referenced in the post for Claude Code / annual subscribers; **not present in the free email body** \u2014 do not invent rebuild steps, source names, or costs beyond what's above.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-002", "U-014"]}, {"n": "29", "subject": "The 8 Ways Slack Eats Your Claude Output", "date": "2026-05-04", "file": "29-the-8-ways-slack-eats-your-claude-output.md", "mechanism_short": "**slack-recap** \u2014 a free user-level Claude Code skill that turns a session into a Slack-safe clipboard paste so markdown doesn\u2019t render as a \u201cJSON blob with extra steps.\u201d **Why:** Pasting Claude output into Slack breaks in eight recurring ways (as named in the post/tests): literal asterisks instead of bold; headings as `# ` hash+space; bracket/paren links that do nothing; code fences that print th", "mechanism_md": "**slack-recap** \u2014 a free user-level Claude Code skill that turns a session into a Slack-safe clipboard paste so markdown doesn\u2019t render as a \u201cJSON blob with extra steps.\u201d\n\n**Why:** Pasting Claude output into Slack breaks in eight recurring ways (as named in the post/tests): literal asterisks instead of bold; headings as `# ` hash+space; bracket/paren links that do nothing; code fences that print the language hint as the first line; pipe tables that collapse into a run-on; plus horizontal rules, checkboxes, and raw angle brackets in the end-to-end trap draft.\n\n**Loop when it fires** (natural phrasing \u2014 \u201cSlackify this,\u201d \u201cCopy for Slack,\u201d \u201cGive me a Slack recap\u201d; no special skill vocabulary required):\n1. Pull work from the current session \u2014 what got built, changed, decisions made, who came up.\n2. Draft a Bottom-Line-Up-Front message at a length tier picked by how many real decisions happened.\n3. Run the draft through a **~200-line deterministic lint** that catches the 8 Slack-mangle traps (~95% coverage claimed for the 8-rule list).\n4. Pipe linted text to **pbcopy**. User Cmd+V. No bot, no app \u2014 message lands in clipboard from the user.\n\n**How he built it (before skill code):** three Claude agents in parallel, each writing a brief to disk, then bundled verbatim as the skill\u2019s reference docs (three files, one skill, no rewriting):\n1. Definitive Slack formatting reference \u2014 what works, what silently breaks, \u201cApply formatting\u201d paste-prompt mechanics, 8-rule lint list.\n2. Robert Cialdini\u2019s seven influence principles in ethical form; dark-pattern version of each named so the skill refuses it (e.g. authority = link the data not name-drop; social proof = name who agreed not invent enthusiasm); plain-language word-swap (utilize/leverage \u2192 use; \u201cin order to\u201d \u2192 to). Assume good intent.\n3. Inventory of existing Slack-related skills + skill-creator conventions so the new skill complements rather than collides.\n\n**Two holding moves:**\n- **Deterministic lint over vibes:** idempotent final pass before pbcopy (run ten times \u2192 converges); end-to-end tested on a draft containing every trap; clipboard round-trip byte-for-byte identical to linted output. Model writes from bundled refs; lint reruns anyway (belt and suspenders).\n- **Ask once, save, reuse:** contacts file `name \u2192 Slack ID` (real member IDs ping; `@username` does not). First time a name is worth tagging, skill asks once (how to copy member ID from profile \u2192 three dots; skip option). Append forever; skip \u2192 plain `@Sarah` fallback + preview warning it won\u2019t ping. One user-editable file.\n\n**Generalized shape (as stated):** parallel research agents \u2192 disk reference docs \u2192 pushy skill description on natural phrasing \u2192 model writes from bundled refs not training memory \u2192 deterministic lint at the boundary \u2192 one per-environment contacts file \u2192 clipboard paste.\n\n**Install (from post):** `git clone https://github.com/SantaJordan/slack-recap-skill.git ~/.claude/skills/slack-recap` \u2014 Claude Code auto-discovers `~/.claude/skills/`. Full source on GitHub: SKILL.md, 200-line lint, three reference docs, contacts template ([SantaJordan/slack-recap-skill](https://github.com/SantaJordan/slack-recap-skill)).", "upgrade_ids": ["U-023"]}, {"n": "30", "subject": "The Dossier: Where AI GTM Begins", "date": "2026-05-02", "file": "30-the-dossier-where-ai-gtm-begins.md", "mechanism_short": "Diagnosis + durable-artifact pattern for account truth before any AI GTM scoring. Opening failure: a derived foreign-key field joining call recordings to CRM accounts was wrong on **42.6%** of records \u2192 **37,000** calls attributed to wrong accounts; every account still showed activity and every dossier looked plausible for weeks. Lesson: plausible \u2260 correct. At typical B2B SaaS clients, five syste", "mechanism_md": "Diagnosis + durable-artifact pattern for account truth before any AI GTM scoring. Opening failure: a derived foreign-key field joining call recordings to CRM accounts was wrong on **42.6%** of records \u2192 **37,000** calls attributed to wrong accounts; every account still showed activity and every dossier looked plausible for weeks. Lesson: plausible \u2260 correct.\n\nAt typical B2B SaaS clients, five systems each know something about customers (CRM, call recordings, billing, product analytics, support tickets) and disagree. Example shape: CRM = \u201cActive\u201d; billing = cancelled last month; product last login = 94 days ago; call system has 12 recordings of which 5 belong to a different company via an unaudited 2019 fuzzy name match. Teams treat CRM as source of truth because it is easiest to query; Jordan ranks CRM **third** in trust.\n\n**Trust hierarchy** (when systems disagree, prefer in this order):\n1. **Customer actions** \u2014 product events, logins, payments, feature usage (behavior).\n2. **Customer voice** \u2014 call transcripts, support tickets, emails they wrote.\n3. **CRM-derived state** \u2014 opportunity stages, account types, lead-source classifications (rep-entered, one step from reality).\n\nLoad-bearing claim from four full corpus builds: CRM shows what the org wants to believe; product shows what is happening. Example: rep marks deal \u201cCustomer\u201d when commission triggers; product never logged in \u2192 product wins.\n\n**Customer dossier** = single sorted timeline per account across every system/event, with provenance on every field and conflicts surfaced (not hidden). Answers: what does every system say about this account, and where do they disagree? It **describes and carries evidence** \u2014 e.g. \u201cbilling shows $4,200 MRR; CRM shows $3,800; gap $400 unexplained\u201d \u2014 it does **not** score, predict, or recommend (\u201cat risk\u201d). Reasoning runs **on top of** the dossier; if scoring bleed into the description, a prompt change forces a full dossier rebuild. Durable description artifact \u2192 cheaper/more accurate downstream churn, expansion, win-loss; wrong artifact \u2192 fiction on plausible data.\n\nCTA names **Dossier Builder** among annual Edge tools and a paid **6-wave methodology** (identity linking, revenue reconciliation, critique trio; catch join errors / self-domain trap / fake-account inflation) \u2014 methodology steps and tool internals are **not in this free email**.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-001", "U-006", "U-022", "U-028"]}, {"n": "31", "subject": "I A/B Tested Exa Against Itself. The Cheap Option Won 9x.", "date": "2026-04-30", "file": "31-i-a-b-tested-exa-against-itself-the-cheap-option.md", "mechanism_short": "An **Exa skill** (plus battle-scarred reference) that packages every Exa product and the hard-won routing/validation rules from months of LinkedIn discovery, company research, and demand scoring \u2014 then A/B-proves which Exa mode actually wins on people-at-company search. **A/B (same 100 companies, goal: MFT Engineers at specific banks):** - **Deep Search:** 96 unique people \u2014 kept returning the sam", "mechanism_md": "An **Exa skill** (plus battle-scarred reference) that packages every Exa product and the hard-won routing/validation rules from months of LinkedIn discovery, company research, and demand scoring \u2014 then A/B-proves which Exa mode actually wins on people-at-company search.\n\n**A/B (same 100 companies, goal: MFT Engineers at specific banks):**\n- **Deep Search:** 96 unique people \u2014 kept returning the same Accenture/HCL consultants across queries (generic \u201cMFT experts on LinkedIn,\u201d not people at the target banks).\n- **Research API:** 880 unique people \u2014 searches people **at** the specified company; when it finds none, says so, then returns similar-company people as bonus leads.\n- Result: **9\u00d7 more results**, **1/5 the cost**, and the \u201cexpensive-looking\u201d option was **52\u00d7 cheaper per contact**.\n\n**Scaled people-search + validation pipeline (84,083 chiropractors):**\n1. Exa people search finds candidates \u2014 **$0.006/search**, **65%** raw match rate.\n2. RapidAPI LinkedIn scraper enriches (headline, location, current company).\n3. Claude Haiku validates \u2014 **$0.0005/validation**, catches the **39%** false positives (wrong John Smith / consultant / similar name, different profession).\n- Full cost: **~$0.04 per validated LinkedIn profile** \u2192 **14,459** validated profiles from 84,083 practitioners.\n\n**What the skill wraps:**\n- Six search types on one endpoint: `instant` (sub-100ms grounding), `fast` (sub-350ms agent lookups), `auto`, `neural`, `deep`, `deep-reasoning` (multi-step research with field-level citations \u2014 **replaces Research API, which sunsets May 1**).\n- `findSimilar` \u2014 URL \u2192 semantically similar pages (used to measure Substack topic saturation).\n- Websets \u2014 NL list building \u2192 verified CSV (starts **$49/month**).\n- Three hosted MCP tools: search, fetch URL as clean markdown, advanced search (category filters + subdomain wildcards).\n- 944-line reference: every param/gotcha, plus underdocumented features (`summary.schema`, `subpages`, `systemPrompt`).\n\n**Gotchas the skill encodes (not in official docs):** `category: \"people\"` required for LinkedIn (else company pages); `includeText` must be a **single-item** array (multi-item fails silently); MCP API key goes in the **URL query param**, not the env var.\n\n**Self-improvement:** on invoke, compare skill \u201cLast updated\u201d header to today; if **>30 days** stale, fetch current docs + GitHub README before answering (not a cron \u2014 skill decides it\u2019s stale and fixes itself). API churn examples called out: `maxCharacters` replaced `numSentences` (Feb); `maxAgeHours` replaced boolean `livecrawl`; `instant` search type appeared.\n\nPattern for the stack: skill for the engineer, Corpus wiki entity for Edge Copilot subscribers, freshness check so neither goes stale.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-003", "U-013", "U-027"]}, {"n": "32", "subject": "597 Attendees From a 3-Minute Screen Recording", "date": "2026-04-26", "file": "32-597-attendees-from-a-3-minute-screen-recording.md", "mechanism_short": "A **pixels-only list extractor** shipped as an Edge Copilot tool (annual install): point it at a screen recording of any list that has no API, no scrapable HTML, and no export (example: 3-minute recording of a conference attendee pane; subject cites **597** attendees). **Pixels-only rule:** if contact data exists only as captured pixels, use a **row-cropping pipeline** \u2014 not whole-frame OCR, and n", "mechanism_md": "A **pixels-only list extractor** shipped as an Edge Copilot tool (annual install): point it at a screen recording of any list that has no API, no scrapable HTML, and no export (example: 3-minute recording of a conference attendee pane; subject cites **597** attendees).\n\n**Pixels-only rule:** if contact data exists only as captured pixels, use a **row-cropping pipeline** \u2014 not whole-frame OCR, and not Gemini.\n\n**Why whole-frame fails (empirically measured):** hand a vision model a frame with ~25 contact rows \u2192 structured JSON \u2248 **58%** accuracy. Attention thins across the long list. Gemini specifically hallucinates plausible fake companies / department names (people who actually work elsewhere). Hallucinated rows look real in the CSV; only sample-verify against original frames catches them.\n\n**Fix:** crop each row into its own image; OCR one at a time \u2192 accuracy \u2248 **99%**. Tradeoff: ~1000 small images vs ~30 big frames; **sub-agent parallelism** keeps wall-clock comparable. Attention at 25:1 is thin; at 1:1 the card has nowhere to drift.\n\n**Broader pattern (beyond OCR):** any batch AI read (search-result pages, record tables, log walls) degrades per-item accuracy and returns confident wrong answers that look right. Pattern: **shrink the unit \u2192 parallelize reads \u2192 merge at the end**.\n\nPublic free text stops before the paywalled seven-phase pipeline / gotcha / paste-into-Claude recipe; those details are not in the email body beyond the teaser.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-012"]}, {"n": "33", "subject": "33-monitor-your-competitor-s-social-posts", "date": "2026-04-26", "file": "33-monitor-your-competitor-s-social-posts.md", "mechanism_short": "**Competitor LinkedIn watcher** \u2014 promoted from a one-off bash script into a wiki-wired skill/tool (Edge Copilot\u2013readable). 1. **First script:** watched **three** public competitor LinkedIn company pages (names redacted in the post). Surfaced product launches, trade shows, hiring signals. Lived in one repo, useful to one person, one time. 2. **Promotion:** wired into the wiki \u2014 not just file moves", "mechanism_md": "**Competitor LinkedIn watcher** \u2014 promoted from a one-off bash script into a wiki-wired skill/tool (Edge Copilot\u2013readable).\n\n1. **First script:** watched **three** public competitor LinkedIn company pages (names redacted in the post). Surfaced product launches, trade shows, hiring signals. Lived in one repo, useful to one person, one time.\n2. **Promotion:** wired into the wiki \u2014 not just file moves. Wrote **three** wiki pages Edge Copilot reads: (a) the skill, (b) the vision idea, (c) the pagination trick. Linked them with **named relationships** (skill \u2192 data source; tool \u2192 pattern) so later `/edge` questions about LinkedIn monitoring or \u201cpictures beat captions\u201d land on the right page. Framing: a bash script is a **leaf** (ship once, lose it); a **skill** is the same code with connections so it can be found.\n3. **Vision find (one production run):** one competitor was running a roadshow (city-by-city events, booths, dates). Text-only reading of posts found **~six** named shows. Turning on vision so Claude read attached **poster images** pulled **twenty-one more** sub-events the captions never named \u2014 ~**4\u00d7** the schedule, inside the JPEG. Stated lesson: companies ship signal in the picture, not the caption; text-only is the press release, the poster is the plan.\n4. **Pagination find:** LinkedIn\u2019s documented company-updates endpoint is officially a **POST**; it also serves **paginated GET** (surfaced from the provider\u2019s own playground). That let a **twelve-month** backfill on one company in one pass.\n5. **Hypothesis gate:** both vision and pagination are tagged **hypothesis**, not proven. Schema won\u2019t promote to **repeated** until failure cases (\u201cwhere they explicitly don\u2019t work\u201d) are written. One production run on one company = hypothesis, not a rule. Pattern library allowed to be wrong on purpose so it doesn\u2019t only collect wins.\n6. **Shipping note:** geeky rebuild copy provided for Claude Code; annual subscribers can install the shipped tool with one command. Post credited **Claude Opus 4.7**, approved by Jordan. Build-log dated April 23, 2026.", "upgrade_ids": ["U-019"]}]};
